The prime ministers of India and Japan met in Tokyo.
India's new Prime Minister Narendra Modi meets his Japanese counterpart Shinzo Abe in Toko on his first important foreign visit since his May election victory to discuss economic and security relations.
Mr Modi is on a five-day trip to Japan to consolidate economic relations with the world's third-largest economic nation.
Plans for stronger nuclear cooperation are at the top of the agenda.
According to reports, India is also hoping for a treaty on defense cooperation between the two nations.
Karratha police arrested 20-year-old after rapid motorcycle chase
A motorcycle was seized after the rider drove it at 125 km/h in a 70 km/h zone and through bushland to escape police in Bilbara.
Traffic police officers in Karratha tried to stop a blue motorcycle this morning after watching it as it left a gas station on Bathdate Road at 125 km/h.
Police report that the driver then ignored the stop signals and continued driving on Burgess Road before turningling into the bushland, where officers lost sight of it.
The motorcycle, as well as a person equivalent to the driver's description, were later seen at a house at Walcott Way in Bulgarra.
Karratha police accuse a 20-year-old man of non-observancement of a stop signal as well as reckless driving.
He is scheduled to appear before the Karratha district court on September 23.
The motorcycle was secured and seized for three months.
George Webster charged with hotel rapes in Naim and Pitlochry
A man is on trial for the rape of women in two hotels.
George Webster, 28, was read the indictment at a hearing by the Glasgow Supreme Court.
He is accused of raping a woman at Scotland's Hotel in Pitlochry in Perthshire on June 7, 2013.
The indictment is that Webster attacked her while she was "uninterested, slept, and could not signal consent."
Webster is also accused of raping a second woman at the Golf View Hotel in Naim in the Scottish highlands on May 4, 2014.
Judge Lady Rae sent the trial date for November 17 at Edinburgh's high court.
Recallusion to the very American ideal of labour rights as human rights
Congressors Keith Ellison and John Lewis have tabled a legislative proposal to establish the organization of unions as a civil right.
"Just as unions die, the middle-class jobs die, too," said Ellison, a Democrat from Minnesota and vice-chairman of the Progressive Caucus in Congress.
That's whhere I proudly present the Employee Authorisation Act together with civil rights icon John Lewis.
This groundbreaking law gives workers the same legal options for discrimination because of the organization of trade unions as with other forms of discrimination - and thus stops anti-unification forces.
The addition of the national employment law law to create a possibility for workers facing discrimination to organize a trade union representation in order to sue for justice in a civil court - and to receive damages or fines - is a meaningful and necessary initiative.
But it's certainly not a radical initiative - at least not according to American standards.
In fact, the best way to understand what Ellison, Lewis and the other sponsors propose to their bill is the connection back to a very American idea.
Despite the setbacks that unions have faced in recent years - in Wisconsin, Michigan and other states across the country - Americans have once encouraged countries around the world to recognize, expand and comply with labor rights.
There was a time that millions of Americans still remember when this country cited democracy, freedom of speech, freedom of press, and the right to association in one breath.
When the United States occupied Japan after World War II, Gen. Douglas Mac Arthur and his body encouraged the country to adopt a constitution designed to ensure that the militarized autocracy of Hedki Tojos would be replaced by democracy.
Aware that the workers and their unions played a role in the creation of a new Japan, they used a language that explicitly acknowledged that "the rights of workers to unification and joint negotiation and action are guaranteed."
As the United States occupies Germany after World War II, General Dwight David Eisenhower and his staff urged the Germans to write a constitution that would ensure that the fascism of Adolf Hitler would be replaced by a strong democracy.
Recognising that the workers would have to organise themselves, and should raise their voices in the new nation, the Germans inserted a clause that explicitly testified: "The right to establish associations in order to ensure and improve the working and economic conditions is guaranteed to each individual and every profession or commerce.
Agreements restricting or aiming to reduce this right are zero and annull; measures aimed at this are unlawful.
When former First Lady Eleanor Roosevelt presided over the International Commission on Human Rights, which drafted the Universal Declaration of Human Rights as adopted by the United Nations in 1948 as a worldwide agreement, Roosevelt and the other authors inserted a guarantee that "everyone has the right to form or accede to trade unions to protect their interests."
For generations, Americans have accepted the basic premise that labour rights are human rights.
When this country advised other countries on how to shape civil and democratic societies, Americans declared that the right to set up a trade union - and that this union should then participate in collective bargaining as an equal partner with companies and government agencies - must be protected.
Sincence these rights came under fire in America, it is indeed very wise to look at the American ideal again, according to which the working population must have the right to unite and make themselves heard in a free and open society.
As pastor Martin Luther King Jr. said fifty years ago:
History is a great teacher.
Now everyone knows that the labor movement did not limit the strength of the nation, but increased it.
By raising the standard of living of millions of people, work has miraculously created a market for industry and raised the entire nation to unimaginable levels of production.
Those who attack work forget these simple truths, but history preserves them.
The story remembers, and that's what we're supposed to do.
Formal recognition of labour rights as human rights - and the extension of the protection of civil rights as protection against discrimination in the creation of workers' representatives - is long overdue.
Keith Ellison and John Lewis are renewing the ideals that made America great in the past and redeemed the promise of democracy.
Richter temporarily halts law that all abortion clinics in Louisiana could force closure
A U.S. federal judge tentatively stopped the implementation of a law in Louisiana on Sunday, which lawyers say presumably led to the closure of all of the state's five abortion clinics.
This measure, brought to law by Bobby Jindal, governor of Louisiana, in June and is scheduled to come into force on Sept 1, would force physicians carrying out abortions to acquire a patient enrollment right at a hospital within a 30-mile radius around their practice.
However, the judge's decision means that doctors can provisionally continue to carry out legal abortions while requestinging such a right.
"The plaintiffs may continue to work in accordance with the law as they strive to obtain these rights," Federal Judge John deGravelles wrote in his ruling.
Within a month, a hearing will be scheduled for the judge to bring about a more permanent decision for the law.
Abortion advocates welcomed the decision, the latest in a series of verdicts against similar measures, and said doctors would thus gain more time to gain hospital admission rights.
"Today's decision means that the women in Louisiana evade a devious law that wants to deprive them of their health and rights," said Nancy Northup, president and chairwoman of the Center for Reproducement Rights, which had sued on behalf of three of the state's five clinics to halt the law.
It was not immediately clear whether the verdict also extended to the doctors from the two clinics, who had not joined the lawsuit and had also applied for admission rights.
Louisiana is one of 11 states that have enacted similar laws, with courts recently declaring such measures in Alabama and Mississippi to be unconstitutional.
Key points of a law in Texas that would have forced most of clinics remaining in the state to closure were halted by a federal judge on Friday.
Abortion advocates and the American College of Ostretricians and Gynecologists, as well as the American Medical Association, conjudicially state that admission rights from doctors require unnecessary restrictions.
Abortion opponents counter that the measures serve to protect women's health, although some also welcomed the effects of closing clinics.
Only one doctor who performs abortions in Louisiana has patient admission rights, the Center for Reproduce Rights said.
If all other doctors in the state are forced to no longever carry out abortions, this doctor would no longer carry out the surgery out of fear for his safety, the group said.
In their reasoning against the verdict, representatives of the state of Louisiana said that they would not punish doctors for carrying out abortions, while their requests for patient instruction rights would still be pending.
Delayed diagnoses and a lack of access to the best treatment options lead to higher death rates from ovarian cancers in rural areas.
Angelina Jolie and her brother James have posted a video hommage for their mother online who died of ovarian cancer in 2007.
For women in the rural areas of Australia there is a higher risk of dying from ovarian cancer than for women in cities.
Researchers analyzed the medical data from more than 1100 Australian women who were diagnosed with ovarian cancer in 2005 and found that only 25 percent were still alive five years after diagnosis.
Research leader Susan Jardan of QIMR Berghofer Medical Research Institute said that for those living in rural and remote regions of the state, the risk of mortality during the study was 20 percent higher than for those in urban areas.
SMALL STUDY: New medicines could slow lung and ovarian cancer
The researchers examined women's medical care over seven years.
Dr Jordan said that a woman's age at diagnosis, the type of ovarian cancer, existing pre-existing conditions, as well as social and economic status, also affected the chances of survival.
For older women and those whose cancers had already progressed at the time of diagnosis, the survival rate was lowest.
For those living in rural and remote regions of the state, the risk of mortality during the study was 20 percent higher than for those in urban areas.
Although the study did not look at why women living outside the city were more likely to die from ovarian cancer, Dr. Jordan suspects a late diagnosis and lack of access to the best treatment centers as possible factors.
"This disease is best treated by gynaecological oncologists, and these are mostly found in larger cities," she said.
Despite the improvement of telemedicinal services in order to oppose the distances, she proposed as a possible remedy a strengthening of the service of flown-in doctors to give specialists the opportunity to take care of women more densely in their place of residence, as well as treatment programs for people outside their region.
Dr Jordan said that regardless of geographical status, the study revealed a poor long-term survival rate in women in ovarian cancer and underlined the need for better treatment and prevention strategies.
Funded by Rio Tinto Ride to Conquer Cancer, the study is published today in the Medical Journal of Australia.
In March 2013, Elisha Neave, a young mother of 33, learned that she had an aggressive form of ovarian cancer.
Garden centres regret declining homeowner numbers
The decline, along with an increased decline in the number of homeowners under the age of 35, could cause garden centres to lose tens of thousands of pounds a year if today's young consumers, according to an HTA study, according to the Financial Times, reach the "nuclear age group for garden products."
The report states that tenants spend an average of 55 percent of the amount that people with homes spend on their garden.
The rise of people living in highly urbanized areas without gardens, the popular procedure of paving front yards for parking purposes and the declining garden size are other factors that threaten the industry, in which an estimated £5 billion annually is implemented in sales.
In Greater London, where the share of homeowners has fallen from 61 to 43 per cent over a period of 6 years, the lowest expenditure on garden products per household is reported.
The HTA and the Royal Horticultural Society point out that housing for rent or small gardens don't mean that people can't draw plants.
Guy Barter, chief garden consultant for the RHS said: "Container gardeners, for example, are very popular with tenants because they can take their plants with them during a relocation."
The HTA report identified the period between 1997 and 2005 as the "golden age" of garden retail due to rising homeowner numbers and economic prosperity from the late 1980s to the mid-1990s.
He also predicted an improved market situation for this year because of the better weather after unfriendly weather conditions in March and April last year.
The protest movement led by opposition politician Imran Khan and clergyman Tahirul Qadri in Pakistan continues.
In the capital Islamabad, around 1000 demonstrators have stormed the state television station PTV.
Information Minister Pervaiz Rashid said soldiers, however, could have pushed the protesters out of PTV buildings again.
The station ceased operations for around 40 minutes, but is now broadcasting again.
At the same time, around 3000 protesters tried to get to Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif's residence.
About a kilometre away, they fought clashes with security forces equipped with sticks and stones.
Police used tear gas.
The demonstrators demanded Sharif's resignation, but he rejects this.
The country's army, meanwhile, calls for a political solution to the conflict "without wasting time and using it without violence."
After a meeting at headquarters on Sunday evening, the military said: "The army remains committed to its role to ensure the security of the state."
The military plays an important role in Pakistan and has already couped more often.
Opposition politician Imran Khan accuses Premier Sharif of cheating in the parliamentary election in May last year.
Protests had escalated over the weekend after Khan and Qadri called on their supporters to advance to Sharif's residency.
Three people were killed.
Turkey orders U.S. diplomat over espionage report
Turkey's Foreign Ministry has summoned the country's longest-serving U.S. diplomat to provide information on a report on American and British espionage in Turkey.
Deputy Prime Minister Bülent said the U.S. embassy's managing director and Turkish officials had discussed Monday's report.
The German magazine Der Spiegel, as well as the online magazine The Intercept, reported that those from the former U.S. analyst of the National Security Agency, Edward Snowden, show that Turkey was a major area of activity for the U.S. and British intelligence agencies.
According to Turkish news agencies, Turkish President Recep Tayyip Erdogan downplayed the importance of the report and said that all the major countries spied on each other.
An earlier report, which was also targeted by Germany’s most important intelligence service, caused a much more angry reaction from the Turkish government.
Warning strike: Locomotives threaten consequences for commuters
The nationwide warning strike of the locomotive drivers on Monday evening could also affect passenger traffic in NRW.
The withdrawal announced for 6 p.m. to 9 p.m. nationwide concerns all transport companies, including long-distance, regional and S-Bahn traffic,
The main direction of the warning strike is freight traffic.
Photograph: A. Burgi/Archive
The district chairman of the trade union of German locomotive drivers (GDL), Sven Schmitte, said in Düsseldorf.
We anticipate significant outages.
There are no regional priorities.
The main direction of the warning strike is freight traffic.
Deutsche Bahn in NRW announced that it would do everything possible to keep the impact as low as possible.
However, the GDL did not say where it wanted to strike, so it was difficult to foresee the consequences concretely.
Magaluf police chief accused of corruption
Prosecutors presented evidence of extortion by police officers and employees of Calvià to the office of the Mallorca prosecutor's office on Friday.
Calvià's chief police on the holiday island of Mallorca have been arrested following allegations of corruption by businessmen and bar owners in the notorious binge-drinking hotspot Magaluf.
Chief commissioner José Antonio Navarro has been taken into custody after allegations of corruption against him by several businessmen from Punta Ballena, the street where Most Magaluf's bars and nightclubs lie.
According to the online daily newspaper Majorca Diario, the accusers submitted evidence of extortion by police and employees of the city of Calvià to the office of Majorca's anti-corruption office on Friday.
Two other local police officers have been arrested by Spain's Guardia Civil in connection with the corruption allegations and will be questioned together with Navarro by a judge.
Spain's national daily ABC reported that the disgruntled nightclub owners testified that the authorities' nepotism had a serious impact on their businesses.
"It's no longer about making money, it's about survival," one of the businessmen told the court.
Leave our livelihood out of the game.
We've got nothing to lose.
Magaluf made international headlines this summer due to a viral YouTube video showing an 18-year-old British tourist performing on 24 men during a suffocation tour Fellatio.
Island authorities have since tried to stop the improper behaviour of alcoholic holidaymakers in Magaluf by minimizing the number of attendees in the notorious alcohol-soaked pub tours.
The Playhouse Club, where the fellatio incident took place, has been forced to close for a year, while Playhouse and pub-touring organizer Carnage must jointly pay a penalty of €55,000 ($73,000).
The tourist resort of Magaluf, which is particularly popular with young British holidaymakers, is also known for a number of accidents under the influence of alcohol, including the trend of "balconing", in which people jump from one balcony to another or from a balcony into the hotel pool.
The first day of spring with wet and stormy weather conditions impairs flights from Adelaide Airport
SPRING has given South Australia a wintry surprise, with heavy showers and strong wind affecting flights from Adelaide Airport.
In the evening until 9 p.m
The last precipitation was the result of a series of shoverel, including a casting that began just before 8 p.m., providing almost 4mm of rainfall in about 10 minutes.
After last week's winter provided for an early dose of spring, temperatures in the city felled back to a high of just 15.8°C on Monday.
Gusty weather conditions have likely led to the delayed landing of a Virgin Airlines flight from Melbourne to Adelaide.
The plane should have landed shortly after 7.30 p.m., but was hit by a shearing wind - a sudden change in wind speed or direction over a short distance - and had to take off.
Gusts of wind reached about 50 km/h on the ground at Adelaide Airport at that time.
The flight data showed that the aircraft also had to take off during a second landing approach and finally set off at about 20:40.
The flight delay subsequently led to the delay of some other flights, including an Emirates flight from Dubai and a Quantas flight from Sydney.
A maximum temperature of 16°C is predicted for Adelaide on Tuesday, with possible showers.
Matthies thinks it’s high time for great networking
In view of galloping technical progress, which threatens to turn our comfortable home into high-tech switching centers, our columnist Bernd Matthies consoles himself with an extremely familiar electronic accessory.
As I'm writing this here, my look at the spiral cable that connects the listener to the phone, and I'm thinking: Boah, how retro is that?
But there is a consideration behind it: If the cable were not, all listeners would fly around the house in no time, and that would be significantly more retro.
