Israeli children return to their schools after the war in Gaza
Thousands of Israeli children from areas near the Gaza Strip returned to schools on Monday after spending the summer in air shelters, while rocket and mortar shelling on their communities during the 50-day war between Israel and Hamas shut down and Gaza schools remained closed as the area recovered from the fighting.
The start of school brought a sense of joy and expectation to congregations marked by rocket bullet holes in southern Israel, but the signs of fighting are still fresh.
In the southern city of Ashdod, colleagues of the "Pashosh" nursery, which was hit by a missile, removed shrapnel from the walls and sliding walls before students arrived.
"We're a bit scared, but we're excited," said Ronit Bart, a resident at Kibbutz Saad and English teacher there.
Many children from our area desperately need to return to a routine.
Her 11-year-old daughter Shani Bart said it felt "a bit weird" about suddenly going back to school.
"These have been difficult times, and we didn't leave the house at all," she said.
President Reuven Rivlin visited Kibbutz near the border with Gaza to offer his support.
Until a ceasefire interrupted the war last week, thousands of residents of the border communities like Saad remained in their homes or left their homes to move into safer areas farther away from Gaza and evade rocket and mortar fire.
Many residents of Nahal Oz, a community near the border with Gaza where a 4-year-old boy was killed by a Palestinian mortar grenade, are still hesitant to return.
The education ministry said about a dozen families hadn't returned yet.
Her children were provisionally housed in other schools.
Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu visited a school in Sderot, a border town with Gaza that is heavily marked by Palestinian shelling.
He appealed to the children to learn a lot and said "we're going to make sure their knowledge accrues and offer you safety."
Israel and Hamas agreed on an indefinite ceasefire last Tuesday.
The ceasefire caused an immediate end to the fighting; however, important problems remain unresolved, such as Hamas's demand for a lifting of the Israeli-Egyptian blockade of Gaza and the reopening of air and seaports in Gaza.
Israel is calling for disarmament of Hamas and handing over the bodies of two Israeli soldiers killed in the fighting.
A new round of indirect talks is expected to begin in Egypt later this month.
In the war, more than 2,100 Palestinians were killed, three-quarters of them civilians, and at least 494 children, according to estimates by the
Israel denies this information and estimates that at least half of those killed are fighters, but did not provide any concrete evidence of this information.
On the Israeli side, 66 soldiers and six civilians, including a Thai worker, were killed.
Hamas and other fighters in Gaza fired 4,591 rockets and mortar shells during the fighting on Israeli cities, most in the country's south.
Meanwhile, the Israeli military carried out more than 5,000 air and other attacks.
The Israeli attacks damaged or destroyed thousands of homes in the Gaza area and about 250,000 people sought refuge in more than 100 U.N. schools remodeled into temporary shelters.
While tens of thousands of people continue to live in the shelters and the fighting is ongoing, the education authorities last week postponed the start of the school year.
"I hope the school is reopened soon so we can continue to learn, just like the children in the world and the Jewish children," said Mohammad Amara, a 13-year-old boy who is currently living in a school in Gaza City.
By election in Saxony: CDU deputy Strobl questions NPD ban procedure
In view of the NPD's loss of votes in Saxony, CDU federal vice-president Thomas Strobl has advocated an end of the ongoing NPD ban proceedings before the Federal Constitutional Court.
Berlin - According to CDU federal deputy Thomas Strobl, the ongoing prohibition proceedings against the NPD should come to an end before the Federal Constitutional Court.
"The NPD scores worse than last time," Strobl, who is also CDU's head of state in Baden-Württemberg, told the "Leipziger Volkszeitung."
This is a pleasing development in the right direction and a development that leads me to the conclusion that the NPD is combated politically and not before the Constitutional Court.
The NPD had narrowly failed in Sunday's vote at the five-percent hurdle.
A first attempt for a ban of the far-right party had failed in 2003, because the constitutional protection at that time also had informants in the NPD leadership.
In December 2013, the Federal Council submitted a new application in Karlsruhe after long preparation.
The federal government and the Bundestag did not agree.
Harburg: Tents for new refugees
At Neuländer Platz, refugees are now housed in tents.
The central initial reception for refugees in Harburg is bursting at the seams.
Now the Interior Authority has placed three Red Cross tents next to it.
The district politicians were only informed shortly before - they are unenthusiastic.
"These Hauruck actions continue to stoke reservations of the citizens and do not give the district politicians any opportunity to participate constructively in the humane accommodation of refugees," says Britta Herrmann, parliamentary group leader of the Greens in the Harburg district assembly.
Her party is demanding that the Harburg district administration currently provide untapped homes for accommodation.
96 refugees can be accommodated in the three tents
Reasons for the overcrowding in the initial reception are the onslaught of those seeking help - and the circumstance that the authorities cannot provide enough places in follow-up facilities.
Many refugees therefore stay longer than the regular three months.
House prices have experienced the sharpest winter rise in seven years, a widely observed index shows.
The RP Data CoreLogic Hedonic Hedonic Home Value Index for homes in the Australian capital rose 1.1 per cent in August, according to Monday's RP data.
As a result of this increase, the total increase in June, July and August is 4.2 percent, the largest increase in the winter months since 2007.
The annual price increase was 10.9 percent, more than twice as high as the increase in the 12 months to August 2013, but the increases weren't evenly spread across the country.
RP Data's head of research, Tim Lawless, said Sydney and Melbourne are powering a two-part market.
RP Data's figures prove Sydney's house prices rising 16.1 per cent last year, up 11.7 per centre in Melbourne, by contrast.
The following markets are Adelaide, Brisbane and Darwin, where prices rose by an average of five and six per cent respectively.
At the other end of the scale is Canberra, which has been hit hard by cuts in government spending and where prices have only risen 1.4 percent over the year.
Mr Lawless went on to say that, with the start of spring, there would now be an increase in properties for sale for the next few months that would subject the market "to a real test."
"If you look at the persistently high rate of auction prices, a generally fast sales rate, and the persistently low interest rate environment, it's likely that home prices will rise even further over the next three months," he said.
In a statement to the Neue Presse, he contradicts statements made by the managing director Peer Medau and attests to posing "like a general".
This is not the style of the university, according to the statement.
For example, the Medau family had already been "orally informed in mid-June about the request of the university.
The statement that the plans only became known in July is definitely wrong," Pötzl said.
At the same time, he points out that the plans are not "pre-determinations or even decisions", but there are "preferences".
The extension of the narrow road up to the castle, which is necessary for the access road, is also less dramatic than feared by Medaus.
There was no mention of a nine-meter-wide road.
According to the current level, it is about 5.50 meters, which is one meter wider than before," explains Pötzl.
Pötzl does not want to accept the argument of the Medau operators that their free and school-owned parking areas will only be used by students of the university in the future.
It is not parked at all, because, as with other public parking spaces, this can now be solved intelligently.
