Houthi rebels blead roads in Yemen's capital
In the conflict between Shiite Houthi rebels and the Yemeni government, the Houthis have called for civil disobedience in the capital of Sanaa.
Houthi supporters occupied central streets in Sanaa on Monday, a spokesman for the Houthi party "Ansar Allah" said.
Access to parliament has also been blocked.
Yemeni media reports of traffic chaos in the capital.
According to the online edition of the "Yemen Post," Houthi leader Abdulmalik al-Huthi had called for the measures on Sunday evening.
Houthi, according to the paper, threatened a further escalation of the conflict should its demands not be met.
The Shiite Houthis are campaigning for more independence from Sunni-ruled Yemen, calling for the government's resignation.
Houthi's tribe has besieged the Yemenite capital for almost two weeks.
Around 30,000 rebels have set up protest camps on the large access roads of Sanaa.
In the surrounding area, there are always bloody battles with the Yemenite army.
Kenya registers officials to curb 'ghost employees'
Kenya has begun to biometrically register all state officials to erase "ghost employees" from the government's payrolls.
Employees who don't register over the next two weeks would no longer be paid, according to a government announcement.
The government suspects that thousands of people will still receive salary payments even after their departure from public service.
President Uhuru Kenyatta, when he took office in 2013, promised to fight corruption in the civil service.
At an audit earlier this year, it was found that at least $1 million (£700,000) per month was lost on payments for "ghost employees" and through other abusive financial practices.
The government suspects salaries will continue to be disbursed on accounts after one person has died or leaves public service, Wanyama Chebusiri reports for the BBC from the capital Nairobi.
All officers must now appear at identification centers over the next two weeks to ensure that their data about the biometric registrations are recorded, has been communicated in a government message.
All those who would not do so without a credible apology would be removed from the pay list, the communication continued.
"Through this approach contributes significantly to the rationalization of public service by determining the actual number of civil servants and also adjusting the payroll at both levels of government - that's how the "ghostic employees" are eliminated," said Anne Waiguru, the cabinet secretary at the Ministry of Decentralization and Planning.
The scandal surrounding art consultant Helge Achenbach will be heard in court for the first time on 11 November.
