HR4-RADTOUR 700 cyclists make stopovers in the spa park of Bad Salzhausen and Schotten
When cyclists rest on their tour in Bad Salzhausen or Scots, this is nothing special in itself.
On Friday, however, only the number of restrats made an impression: The 700 participants of the hr4 bike tour including escorts enjoyed a break in the lower spa park of Bad Salzhausen and later an open-air party in the Schottener Alteburgpark.
Under the motto "Cycling and partying on newly discovered paths between Nidda and Kinzig", the participants had met on Thursday in Bad Vilbel and witnessed a concert by Van Baker & Band there.
On Friday it became serious: The cyclists tackled the first stage, the 60 kilometers over Florstadt to Bad Salzhausen and on to Schotten.
In Bad Salzhausen, the athletes were greeted by mayor Hans-Peter Seum, who took part by bicycle in Bad Vilbel, and the head of Bad Salzhausen's in-house company Petra Schwing-Döring.
In order to refresher the body and mind, all sorts of good things had been prepared: The rural women from Ranstadt and Nieder-Mockstadt had baked 85 sheets of apple, plum, sprinkles and cheesecakes, which wonderfully mouthed the capable cyclists.
"Such cakes can only bake rural women," praised one participant from Aulendiebach, while ordering another piece.
At the beverage stand of the gymnastics club Geiß-Nidda, cold refreshments could be purchased.
The "Duo Bravo" entertained the visitors with well-known melodies from operettas and musicals and garnered enthusiastic applause.
The beautiful ambience of the park seemed relaxing.
In addition, a gentleman enjoyed a head massage from his partner: "He deserved it, after all, he has already reattached jumped bicycle chains today".
Meanwhile, the mayor and the plant manager still had to work: They were interviewed at the fountain in front of the drinking space by reporter Rainer Janke and took the opportunity to promote Bad Salzhausen on the radio.
Doctoral director Tobias Hagen, since the first hr4 bike tour in 2004 as tour leader, was only able to snort for a short time, since he had plenty to do with the technology on site.
His pick-up truck, in which he communicated by means of three telephones and a radio with his employees and the 15-member motorcycle squadron of the Hesse police, wrote Facebook entries and made reports, also needed a check.
"The majority of the participants come from the Rhine-Main area," he said, "but cyclists have also arrived from Giessen or Fulda, Lauterbach or Taunus and even from numerous cities in Westphalia.
The number of participants depends a bit on the available accommodation capacities.
In category I the participants are accommodated in hotels, in category II in collective accommodation.
From the health resort, the cyclists had a good impression when, after an hour and a half, they broke up again: "Bye!"
"It was beautiful in Bad Salzhausen!" they waved goodbye.
After arriving in Scots, the group was allowed to take a brief breather.
But in the evening, the cyclists were challenged in the idyllic Alteburgpark.
Under the direction of the Scottish Society for Tourism and City Marketing (GTS), the park had received a nice festive ambience and, of course, one had prepared treats, for example flambées with well-chilled feather whiters.
Mayor Susanne Schaab, GTS Managing Director Markus Hoßfeld and MSC Chairman Wolfgang Wagner-Sachs welcomed the visitors in his function as member of the Executive Board of the ADAC Hessen-Thuringia.
The three were visibly pleased with the "many operations in Scots".
DJ Tobias Hagen then released the dance stage.
The opportunity for sweeping movement in the open sky was eagerly exploited despite the sporting activity on the bike.
Among the guests, there were increasingly citizens who were impressed by the good mood as well as the big crowd of cyclists.
On the second day for the cyclists, the stations Birstein, Wächtersbach and Bad Orb - including a concert with the Trenkwalders - were on the program.
Yesterday, after 180 kilometers of cycling, the athletes arrived again in Bad Vilbel.
