Berkeley declares housing market 'normal' again
One of London's most prominent contractors pointed out that the housing market in south-eastern England had "returned" back to normal levels.
Homes in the capital were highly sought-after and the subject of huge price rises, prompting the Bank of England to set caps on mortgage lending in June due to widespread fears of a credit bubble.
Tony Pidgley, founder and chief executive of high-class construction firm Berkeley, said Monday: "Since the start of the current financial year, the market has returned to normal transaction levels since peaking in 2013," adding that this provides a "stable environment for action."
London's real estate market was well-positioned during the downturn as foreign buyers pushed into the capital.
According to information from the cadastral office, prices in the urban area have skyrocketed 18.5 per centuries in the past year alone, far more than the average increase in England and Wales as a whole.
Average selling prices at Berkeley's private, low-cost and student offerings rose by about one-fifth last year and were around £423,000 at the end of April.
A stronger pound, however, in recent months has made real estate ownership in London less attractive to foreign buyers - some of which have also been deterred by introducing new property taxes and political rhetoric around a possible "villa tax" ahead of the general elections next May.
Last week's London-based real estate broker Foxtons warned that the mortgage market report for April, in which sharper lending arrangements were unveiled, would also lead to lower market growth in both property sales and prices in the second half of the year.
New data from the Bank of England from Monday shows a decline in the mortgage permit in July, which also suggests that the real estate market is cooling.
Hamptions International, another real estate agency, corrected its forecast for the rise in property prices in London for 2015 down to 3 per cent, based on an already weakening house price awareness.
The volume of transactions in London's most expensive areas, such as Chelsea, Mayfair and Kensington, meanwhile, has declined by a quarter year after year, according to broker WA Ellis.
Still, the desire for a home in the capital is a blessing for Berkeley, as the amounts due for schedule sales amount to more than £2.2 billion.
Mr Pidgley added: "Demand for the right, well-planned product in the best locations remained stable and, as a result, futures sales have also remained stable."
In June, the company reported that it had sold 3,742 new homes in the year to the end of April - almost a third more than at its peak in 2007 before the crisis.
Annual pre-tax earnings rose 40 per cent annually to £280m, sales 18 per cent to £1.6 billion.
In a notice Monday ahead of the company's annual meeting, Mr Pidgley said revenues for Berkeley in the current year were expected to meet current market expectations.
The analysts consistently estimate the annual pre-tax result at £450m.
Berkeley shares stood firm at £23.96 in London trading this afternoon.
