Lesotho military member says no coup is planned; prime minister stays in South Africa
Official military officials in Lesotho denied planning a coup to overthrow the government and said they were cracking down on police officers accused of arming political fanatics.
Prime Minister Thomas Thabane fled the country and announced that the country's army had surrounded his official residence and occupied government buildings in the capital Maseru.
The Premier brought his family to adjoining South Africa after reporting on assassination threats.
Military spokesman Major Ntlele Ntoi said there was de facto no coup, but that the military was responding to the threat posed by "political fanatics" whose armament was imminent by the police.
"What happened this morning was that Lesotho's staff acted after receiving several intelligence reports that within the police some elements that actually wanted to arm some of the political, partisan-polistical young fanatics who were on the verge of wreaking havage," he told Voice of America.
South African government spokesman Clayson Monyela said the military action had givened the impression of an overthrow.
"Even though no one has claimed to have seized the government by violence, all reports agree that the activities of Lesotho's defense forces so far show signs of a coup," he said.
Spokesmen for the Lesotho military announced on Sunday that the soldiers were retreating to their barracks and that the situation in the capital was quiet.
In the meantime, Deputy Prime Minister Mothetjoya Metsing is taking over the governance during Thabane's absence.
Thabane said he believes he's become a target because of his attempt to fight corruption in the country.
There has been great tension in Lesotho since June after Thabane suspended parliamentary sessions due to clashes in his unity government.
He said his actions had not undermined the government, despiting other accusations.
Production in the eurozone at a 13-month low
The increase in production in the eurozone slowed to the lowest point of the past 13 months in August, according to a closely-observed study.
The final Markit Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI) fellowed to 50.7 in August, up from 51.8 in July.
An indication over 50 indicates an expansion.
New orders declined and factories experienced slumps at a time of rising tensions between the EU and Russia over Ukraine.
The figures will arrive ahead of the European Central Bank (EZB) meeting on Thursday.
Markets expect a clear plan from the bank to deal with a hesitant recovery from the eurozone as well as for the looming deflation at an inflation rate of just 0.3%.
There is speculation that the head of the ECB, Mario Draghi, could provide further information later this week on whether he is considering quantitative relief for the eurozone, according to those carried out by Britain and the US during the financial crisis.
"Although small growth is better than no growth at all, the curbing impact of increasing economic and geopolitical uncertainties for manufacturers has become clearer," said Rob Dobson, lead economist at Markit.
Factory PMI for Germany, Russia's largest trading partner in the EU, fellowed to its lowest level in 11 months at 51.4.
Meanwhile, in the bloc's second-largest economic nation, France, the PMI fell to 46.9.
France remains a child of concern, as does Italy's relegation from solid expansion to stagnation.
Evidence that incentives for growth is no longer taking hold in Germany's key industry mechanism, as well as in Spain and the Netherlands, is also not reassuring," Mr Dobson said.
The decline in the industry presumably fuels the fire of analysts expecting further monetary or fiscal incentives.
Positive news came from the Republic of Ireland, where the PMI rose to 57.3, the highest level since the end of 1999.
Howard Archer, chief economics expert at IHS Global Insight, said: "The best thing you can say for the manufacturing industry's manufacturing industry from August is that the sector continues to grow afterwards."
He added: "Producers in the eurozone are obviously finding life quite difficult at the moment, as current increased geopolitical tensions - especially in relation to Russia and Ukraine - increase uncertainties in the continuing difficult conditions in many countries.
These increased uncertainties have clearly affected the economy - in particular, and consumer confidence, and presumably causes some orders to be delayed or even cancelled, particularly large-scale orders.
He said that it was "increasingly likely" that the ECB would eventually have to usher in a type of QE, "although we suspect this will be limited."
