4 tips for better underwater photos and videos
If you would like to take photos or videos underwater, there is a selection of devices.
The cheapest option is a waterproof point-and-shoot, such as the Nikon Coolpix AW120 or an action cam like the GoPro Hero3+ Silver Edition, both of which are available for about $300.
I took these photos at a multi-camera family festivality, all of which are available at less than $350.
Regardless of the equipment, there are a few rules for the best results.
Check your equipment twice.
Even if you have a waterproof camera, make sure that the battery compartment and other compartments are tightly sealed.
Also set your camera to the photos or videos you want to take.
Some cameras and camcorders have scene and shooting modes that optimize exposure time for the dusky underwater conditions.
And before jumping in, check to what depth your equipment is functional.
Some cameras are only approved for up to 5 feet, others up to 50 or 60 feet.
Search for digital cameras for conventional and waterproof models in our shopping guide and reviews.
Take multiple shots - because many won't work out.
Point-and-shoot cameras have an LCD that lets you compose images, action cams don’t usually.
Even if you have an LCD, it’s difficult to look at this underwater, and the correct picture setting is a matter of luck.
So make a lot of shots.
If your camera has a series mode that takes a number of shots with slightly modified exposure settings, take advantage of it.
Stay close to the surface.
The light gets dramatically worse the further you move away from the surface.
So if possible, you will remain close to the surface if you take shots in a pool, a lake or the sea.
As a result, you can also bring more color into your photos; the deeper you go, the less color you will see.
Get close to the objects.
This is also good advice on land, but it is all the more important underwater because of the poor light conditions.
It’s especially important if you’re working with an action cam: these devices often have a fixed wide-angle lens, which means you’ll need to get closer to your objects if they’re supposed to fill out the entire picture frame.
Andrew Lawson was a man of the kind whose character changes things, even in such a gigantic organization as the NHS.
As an advisory anesthesiologist, he dedicated his career to the task of sparing the sick both the agony of the disease and the torment of treatment.
Among his patients, his wife recalls, was an MI6 employee who had to live with the devastating effects of torture.
Lawson knew that the diagnoses and illnesses are important to the doctors, while the patients are predominantly preoccupied with something completely different: pain.
One day in 2007, however, he was the one whose path of suffering began.
"I didn't feel," he wrote in May this year.
I've struggled to pick myself up.
As he struggled with the flu-like symptoms, he noticed that he impatiently blamed his wife Juliet.
"I'd rather want everything immediately than later," he noted.
When Juliet was on a business trip for a week, Lawson felt quite unusual and troubled for no reason.
There was something in the air.
He asked a colleague to do an X-ray examination of his
Only two weeks ago he had been skiing in the French Alps.
The result of the X-ray examination was available.
He suffered from mesothelioma, an incurable cancer that attacks the nipple, i.e. the skin covering the lungs.
As with most cancers, the exact trigger is usually difficult to determine.
For example, while some smokers suffer from lung cancer, not all lung cancer patients used to be smokers.
Mesothelioma is different.
In almost all cases, the trigger is an asbestos load - contact with the fiber building material, which was once considered "miraculous substance" but has now been detected as deadly.
For most of us, mesothelioma is a condition that can be ignored how easily.
Asbestos, after all, is a product of the past.
The most dangerous type of asbestos has not been used in the UK since the 1960s, when the industry voluntarily pledged to stop using it.
Even where it was still used, only people in certain industries worked directly with the fabric - plumbers, construction workers, carpenters and shipyard workers, for example.
As an industrial hazardous substance from another time, it hardly seems worrying today.
But that complacency is inappropriate.
Britain, it seems, is experiencing the culmination of a mesolthelioma epidemic today.
There are more deaths from mesothelioma here than in any other country in the world.
With a total of 2,500 deaths per year, twice as many people die from this disease than in road accidents.
Annual figures of mesotheliomate deaths since 1980 and expected future deaths in the UK
The reason why we are experiencing the deadly effects now, even though asbestos has been illegal for years (1999, all asbestos types have been banned) is that mesothelioma only develops after decades.
And the scourge of mesothelioma is not limited to former workers of the construction industry.
Asbestos was, and often still exists, in the houses where we live, the offices where we work, the schools where we learn and the shops where we shop.
And that's why mesothelioma doesn't know any class barriers, financial limits, professions or age.
The privileged bastions, from the elegant department stores in London to private schools, are not a shelter.
The parliament buildings are asbestous.
Even in the hospitals, where we are supposed to be healed, have already been identified as the curfew of this deadly carcinogen.
Andrew Lawson wasn't old.
He wasn't a plumber, either.
In fact, it was difficult for him to imagine where he might possibly have come into contact with asbestos.
Then he figured it out.
"It looks like there was a lot of asbestos in the tunnels at Guy's Hospital, where I've been training for six years," he wrote.
All - students, nurses, doctors and gatekeepers - use the tunnels.
One wonders how many my peers will suffer from the same disease?
Unfortunately, he was able to answer this question at least in part.
"Of the four doctors who have been educated at Guy's Hospital and who have contracted mesothelioma for the past five years," he wrote in a letter in 2010, "I am the last one still alive."
How many of us are going ill?
Andrew Lawson has been diagnosed with mesothelioma at the age of 48.
When he died on February 17 this year, he was 55.
It was an unusually long period of survival.
Fifty percent of all mesotheliomer patients die within 8 months of diagnosis.
The disease always ends fatally.
We can therefore only repeat Lawson's question: "How many of us are still falling ill?"
Britain's leading expert on mesothelioma, Professor Julian Peto, assumes that between 1870 and 2050, when the asbestos epidemic is expected to come to a standstill, a total of 90,000 will have fallen victim to the disease.
Most of them still have no idea at the moment that they will die like this.
An asbestos mine in Quebec, Canada Alamy
A quick look at the files of the courts, to which those affected often turn because of claims for damages, shows how far the scourge of mesothelioma is already widespread.
In June this year, for example, Marks & Spencer admitted negligently experting asbestos to Janice Allen.
She worked for the chain for nine years, from 1978 to 1987 and was in charge of the clothing department in two locations - one of them the Flagshipstore on Oxford Street.
Mrs. Allen was only 18 years old when she began working at M&S.
Now she has two children in her twenties.
"Before this thing," she says, "I'd never heard of mesothelioma, I didn't even really know anything about asbestos.
I'd never think I'd be concerned.
There are few people who know a lot about asbestos.
The fact is that the term asbestos does not refer to a substance, but a group of six minerals.
The name stems from the word "asbestiform", a description of the fiber structure that makes the fabric resistant and flexible.
Three of the six were used in the construction industry.
Chrysotile, also known as white asbestos, is now by far the most commonly found asbestos in buildings.
It has been used for roof slabs, floor slabs, pipe insulations, boiler insulations, even for brake pads in cars.
It is less deadly than other forms of asbestos, but is nevertheless considered a "great health risk" by the EU and the WHO with a possible consequence of death.
More dangerous, however, is brown asbestos (amosite) and blue asbestos (crocydolite).
Britain was once the world's largest importer of brown asbestos, and experts suspect that "there are clear but indirect hints that this was a main reason for the uniquely high mesothelioma rates."
An employee of Marks & Spencer has been exposed to asbestos at the flagship store on Oxford Street, London
Janice Allen may not have seen himself as a typical mesothelioma victim, but Julian Peto’s work suggests that her story is by no means unusual.
He has presented a study of those affected that "a significant proportion of mesotheliomas without known exposure by work or in-house presumably caused by asbestos contact in the environment."
According to him, much of these contacts is based on the "normal use and aging" of our buildings.
It looks like no one can expect to be safe.
A report by Goddard Consulting, which examined the Palace of Westminster, shows how people, even in the heart of the government, may have been unwittingly exposed to the substance.
In 2009, Goddard reported that the service shafts and pipines behind Parliament's committee rooms were contaminated with asbestos whose deadly fibers could be swirled up by something as harmless as "strong air movements."
Members of parliament are often accused of taking care of their own interests, but here it looks like the opposite is true.
While the Directorate of the Parliamentary Labour Service insisted that the Westminster Palace had received a "clear health certificate," it is now undisputed that −1 billion £ of −work over several years must be incurred to renovate Parliament, modernise the electrical installations and remove the asbestos, and that after the 2015 general elections, MPs are more likely to meet at the nearby QE2 conference centre rather than on the
The Goddard report also pointed out that "the presence of asbestos has not been handled according to the respective provisions."
No one can know if this mismanagement will cost
All that can be done now is wait.
One person who could never accurately determine contact with asbestos is Graham Abbott, a general practitioner.
Like Andrew Lawson, 50-year-old Abbott suspects he was exposed to asbestos during his work in hospitals.
"I worked in a hospital where asbestos lawsuits were decided positively," he says, "but I can't prove it in my case.
It's so difficult to remember all the places you've worked about and the data.
What he still remembers exactly is the day at the beginning of December 2009, when he got such a kind of fever.
He was 45, and performed an operation just late at night.
All of a sudden I had chills.
It came all of a sudden.
I've been feeling terrible.
I didn't think I'd make the ride home anymore.
As a doctor, Abbott knew that the pain from the chest fur, the skin around his lungs.
But like Janice Allen, he had no reason to suspect a mesothelioma.
He didn't go to work for a month.
Amazed doctors took X-rays of his chest and apply pleural cathether to extract fluid from the lungs and test in the laboratory.
However, his ailments were without a diagnosis.
His health slowly improved, and he returned to his workplace.
From time to time, however, the symptoms returned, often after exercise and severe breathing.
In 2011, a patient Abbotts with similar symptoms arrived at his practice and was later diagnosed with mesothelioma.
But Even now Abbott didn't associate this with his own case.
After all, his patient was a few decades older and had worked directly with asbestos.
The connection in the case was clear.
In September 2011, Abbott's condition deteriorated again and his family doctor brought his CT scans and X-rays to a group of experts.
In December 2011, exactly two years after Abbott felt sick for the first time, a probe equipped with a camera was inserted into the cave between the rib and chest fur.
I'm an optimist.
I usually just move on," he says.
Honestly, I hadn't worried that much.
But Rachel, my wife, was worried.
The result of the biopsy in the week between Christmas and New Year was: "I was told that it was mesothelioma."
Graham Abbott Diagnosis Mesothelioma
Suddenly Abbott had appointments with Macmillan nurses, one of whom advised him to take a lawyer.
It was only now that he understood the extent of the epidemic.
"It turned out that asbestos was very widespread, especially in large public buildings where pipes were often insulated with asbestos," he says.
The people exposed to asbestos in these buildings are now falling ill.
And so mesothelioma now meets younger people who have not worked in the typical professions.
The most dangerous pipes with asbestos insulation in hospitals were underground, so patients were presumably rather not exposed to the substance.
But many employees who switched from one building to another in pedestrian tunnels (such as Andrew Lawson) or eat in underground canteens (as Graham Abbott often did), most likely came into contact with the toxic substances.
It turns out that over several decades after the war hospitals may have been life-savaging for the patients, but life-threatening for the attending doctors.
He's still being removed today.
Pupils turned off their Bunsen burners on asbestos mats.
It doesn’t just affect hospitals.
Asbestos has often been used in offices, shops, libraries and town halls due to its fabulous insulating and flame retardant properties.
Schools Too.
In fact, a lot of people probably had the first asbestos contact in the classroom.
Everywhere in the country, in thousands of hours of chemistry, pupils have parked their Bunsen burners on asbestos mats.
The topic of asbestos in schools is now often addressed on websites.
And meanwhile, such innocent objects in our homes, such as floor slabs or shed roofs, contained asbestos by default.
"It's an industrial venom that's included in large parts of our properties," notes Andrew Morgan, the lawyer who represented Andrew Lawson in his trial of Guy's Hospital.
In one case, the only contact that occurred to an affected woman was the demolition of a garden shed in the 1970s.
So take care of how to tear off your garden shed.
The effects of the diagnosis, and the knowledge that the disease is incurable, is immense.
"It takes a while to process that," says Graham Abbott.
I went back to work and tried to move on, but I realized I couldn't focus on what I was doing.
I've been in practice for two weeks.
Then I knew I had to leave and take care of myself.
Well, I'm not going to see another Christmas.
With the greatest difficulty was the change of perspective from the position of a doctor to that of a patient.
Like countless patients before him, he remembers how overwheered he was of all the information he had to cope with.
"It's been very hard to record all of this," he says.
"I asked my doctor "how much longer do I have?"
And they gave me another 12 months.
I remember thinking, "Well, I'm not going to experience another Christmas.
That's it then."
Mesothelioma is particularly insidious because it is our life support mechanism - breathing itself - that causes the deadly cancer.
Most cases of mesothelioma are caused by asbestos contact.
Asbestos is made of the smallest fibers.
If the asbestos is swirled and the fibers are inhaled, they can become stuck in the pistoral fur, the skin around the lungs.
Asbestos fibers irritate the chest fur and can trigger cell mutations.
"The problem arises with the inhaled needle-shaped asbestos fibres," according to Professor Tom Treasure, a heart-thorax doctor who moved to Guy's Hospital in 2001.
Exactly the hospital Andrew Lawson suggests it has exposed him to asbestos is now ironically, a leading center in the treatment of mesothelioma.
Treasure knew Lawson and treated several others who were probably also exposed to asbestos during her training in the hospital.
