By nature, it's invasive right from the start.
The usual options for other cancers don't work as well with mesothelioma.
The effectiveness of surgery, for example, is hotly controversial.
Some people think she's worth a try.
Treasure denies that.
"You can't cut out the chest fur," he says.
You can't get around it with the knife.
Meanwhile, the cancer "doesn't respond very well to chemotherapy," the "effect shows," but doesn't heal.
"Sometimes there are long-term survivors," Treasure says.
But in the end, they're all dying.
Luckily, some patients actually live long, much longer than expected.
Author Stephen Jay Gould died 20 years after diagnosis.
Two-and-a-half years after his own diagnosis, Graham Abbott is still struggling.
Having contacted Mesothelioma, Abbott came into contact with Andrew Lawson, who had become a one-man support and counseling office for co-affected persons four years after his diagnosis.
"Hello, cancer center," he cheerfully envisioned during calls.
