"We had an advantage because I didn't have to be re-executed, I just called and got an appointment," Graham admits.
A normal patient would have to receive a referral and have to wait for approval of treatment.
Money is crucial for mesothelioma sufferers in order to perceive the best available therapies.
But when these treatments eventually inevitably fail, many sufferers stand from a different financial concern - about the future of the families they leave behind.
And so they turn to the courts in the hope of compensation.
Andrew Lawson contacted Andrew Mangar of Field Fisher Waterhouse LLP.
"It's been known since 1898 that asbestos is extremely dangerous for health," Morgan says.
But what changed in the 1960s is that it was recognized that even a very low burden posed a health risk.
And that's where corporate negligence began to play a role.
Andrew Lawson and Guy's Hospital eventually attached their case, but it wasn't what Morgan calls "full-fledged compensation," as Lawson couldn't prove with absolute certainty that his mesothelioma was due to an asbestos contact at Guy's.
However, following the investigation into his death, a spokesman for Guy's confirmed that "the asbestos fibers in the affected basement area were removed in the 1990s."
It was too late for Andrew Lawson.
How asbestos can be removed
