In response, there have been significant new legislation this year that makes it easier for mesothelioma sufferers to claim compensation, even if their former employers are no longer located.
The law has set up £350 million fund funded by the insurance industry for anyone diagnosed after July 2012 can prove a contact but can't sue anyone.
In these cases, the plaintiffs will receive 80 per cent of what a court could have awarded to them in a normal damages process - about £120,000.
Approximately 300 successful applications to the programme are expected each year.
Andrew Morgan, like so many mesothelioma sufferers, thinks that £350 million represented a "very good job" of the insurance industry.
"That's a deal from insurers for insurers," he says, suggesting that this sum is a quarter of what insurers would have had to pay if time hadn't gone over it and Mesothelioma sufferers could find the companies and sue them normally.
Mike Penning, the then employment and pensions minister, also admitted that the law was "not perfect."
But both Penning and Morgan admitted that, in the face of seven fatalities on a daily basis, swift action was necessary.
"People suffer so much and need help today," Penning said during the second hearing of the Mesotheliom law in December last year.
At that time, Graham Abbott had been in the hands of Prof. Loic Lang-Lazdunski for 19 months.
