China denies Hong Kong right to government election, protesters swear retribution
On Sunday, despite increased calls for democratic reforms, the Chinese parliament decided against nominating candidates for the 2017 elections by the Hong Kong electorate.
This crackdown presumably fuels the long-announced protests in Hong Kong’s business district, while activists initiated planning and mobilization within a few hours of the announcement.
The decision of the Chinese People's Congress basically allows the communist leaders to dismiss all non-beijing-loyal candidates.
"That doesn't come unexpectedly, but it makes us angry after all," said legal expert Emily Lau, leader of the Democratic Party.
This isn't what Beijing promised.
They lied to the people of Hong Kong.
And it's clear we're dealing with an authoritarian regime.
In his defense of China's decision, Li Fei, deputy secretary-general of the National People's Congress Standing Committee, said that the admission of public nominations in elections would be too "chaotic" for the Hong Kong government.
Since 1997, when Britain returned control of Hong Kong to the Chinese, Beijing has pledged to let residents elect the government presidency from 2017 onwards.
The Chinese leadership presented Sunday's decision as a democratic breakthrough because there is a direct right to vote for Hong Kong citizens, but the decision also makes it clear that the Chinese leadership is firmly in the hand through a Beijing-controlled nomination committee.
According to a new clause, only candidates "with love for the country and love for Hong Kong" should be admitted.
The decision follows a summer in which some of the largest and most public-effective protests took place in Hong Kong for several years.
Behind the pro-democracy campaign in Hong Kong is the Occupy Central With Love and Peace movement, whose organizers have threatened to seal off the financial district if Beijing does not allow a real universal right to vote.
On Sunday evening, within hours of the pronouncement, hundreds of Occupy Central supporters had gathered in the rain outside Hong Kong's government headquarters.
During the demonstration, organizers said their movement had reached a new stage of civil disobedience and they were planning waves of protest in the coming weeks.
However, they did not provide further details, apparently to avoid problems with the authorities.
In an online announcement, the organizers said that the movement "considered the occupation of the central district only as a last resort, an action that should only take place if all the chances of dialogue are exhausted and there is no other choice.
We are sorry to say that today all the chances of dialogue have been exhausted and the occupation of the central district will definitely take place.
Authorities in Hong Kong have been preparing for Beijing's announcement for days, and tightened security measures including police forces and barricades took place outside the government building on Sunday.
The unrest is compounded by the feeling of many in Hong Kong that they are slowly losing control of their city.
The influx of people from the mainland intensifies competition for products and services.
In addition, the fear is growing that the values of Hong Kong such as democracy and freedom of speech begin to crumble under the growing pressure of Beijing.
Some criticised the demonstrations of the Occupy-Central movement as it endangered business operations - Hong Kong's lifeline.
"The protests they speak of could cause great economic damage depending on how many are involved in it and how long they last," said legal expert Regine Ip, who has long criticized the movement.
We don't want to stoke fears that Hong Kong will spiral out of control.
This impression is bad for investments.
China's state-controlled media also recently reported democracy activists in Hong Kong as subversive agents controlled by Western powers.
This summer, activists organized an unofficial referendum on electoral rights attended by 780,000 people - more than a fifth of Hong Kong voters.
In July, tens of thousands took part in one of the largest demonstrations of democracy in regional history.
The tradition of graduating balls in the U.S
We look forward to responding to this demand and offering the UK's first luxurious travel service to today's students.
In order to achieve a particularly impressive appearance at the university, the company also offers transport options such as private aircraft, Rolls-Royce Phantom, Aston Marten or McLaren P1.
Mr Stewart also stated that the service had a security division.
This service is an ideal alternative for students who would otherwise have to coach their belongings through the country in a dangerously cluttered car.
Together with our new VIF options, we look forward to making sure students reach the university this year with a minimum of effort and maximum luxury.
A company spokesperson said there were no new bookings yet as the service had only just been introduced, but added that "students would book the service in the next few weeks."
The company also said it was expecting a market for this offering despite the tuition fees of £9,000 a year.
Students today are very different in their expectations and demands from the students 10, 20, 30 or 40 years ago - it's more important than ever to make a great first impression, and with VIF you can do just that.
The National Student Union, however, criticised the service as withdrawn.
Megan Dunn, vice-president of the NUS for higher education said: "This offer seems completely separate from the lives of the majority of students.
Many students who start their studies this month are exposed to a crisis of the cost of living, as the available grants in the form of loans and scholarships don’t keep paced with the sharply rising bills for the simplest livelihoods before they can even think of spending thousands of pounds on something as simple as the arrival in student housing.
Feminists criticise race issue and police behavior after Ferguson
The unarmed teenager was killed earlier this month by a police officer in Ferguson, Mo.
Participants lifted their hands into the air and chanted "hands up, don't shoot" as they waited before the funeral.
After two weeks of protesting in Ferguson, Mo because of the shooting of unarmed teenager Michael Brown, blogger Miriam Zoila Perez noticed a change in the online discussions of white feminists.
According to her experience, white feminist commentators put gender before race when it comes to appealing reproductive law and income inequalities.
However, as tension in the Midwest rose and garnered attention across the country, Perez saw that the responses of white women revolved 100% around the breed.
Compared to the responses of black women shortly after the August 9 shots, the personal essays with titles such as "Thoughts to Ferguson of a White Woman" and "Feminism doesn't just revolve around the oppression of women" came relatively late.
But for Perez, that meant an important change.
"I don't think you're finding a lot of people in feminism who are about the white and talking privileges in such a high-class way," she said.
People felt urged to say something about the incidents.
It's characteristic of the crisis we're in and because the situation in Ferguson is so ethnized they have to express that.
For English professor Koritha Mitchell of Ohio State University, Ferguson brought to light problems to which black American women are exposed every day, but who aren't considered "women's themes" in cultural spaces.
"I can post something funny on Facebook about myself and my partner and get 150 likes from anywhere," Mitchell said.
When I post something about how people of color are under siege in their own country, the silence is deafening.
"How can it be safe for women to talk publicly about a relationship, but doesn't talk about security in public spaces?" she asked.
For black women like Mitchell, who have studied race, gender and sexuality in U.S. history, there is no dichotomy between racial and gender problems.
For them, black women haven't had the luxury of neatly separating themes; they live every day in combined reality.
While white women are now uniting the issues of race and gender in feminist mainstream circles, Angela Hattery, a professor of women's and gender studies at George Mason University, says her predecessors did the opposite.
"Between 1865 and 1890, at least 10,000 black men were lynched and the justification was almost always the rape of a white woman," Hattery said.
White women had to be involved in history to justify the lynching.
For Hattery, the division between white and black women came to the point where white female fighters for women's suffrage like Susan B. Anthony analyzed the country in the late years of the 19th-century and found that the fight for the right to vote could only be successful for one group at a time: women or blacks.
"They made the decision to stand up for women's suffrage, and voting rights for black people should come later," Hattery said.
When one remembers such things, with power you become aware of why colored women don't trust white women.
We didn't do our job well.
We've never helped black women protect their husbands and sons.
Even after women were given the right to vote in 1920, it took another decade for white women to organize themselves against lynching in the Association of Southern Woman for the Prevention of Lynching.
The group originated nearly 40 years after the original request for help from black women, Mitchell says.
For Mitchell, the tide of white feminist reactions to Ferguson's questions - police behavior, racial discrimination - is a reflection of the time in which white feminists "catchers" played with the problems that black women have been grappling with for generations.
“Wouldn’t it be great if the activists, who have such traditional feminist platforms have the right to educate a child as seriously as they do with the right to contraception,” Mitchell asked.
Second-generation feminists who built their careers on the struggle for reproductive rights and access to contraceptive means are now trying to understand what that means.
Well-known white feminist activist Gloria Steinem went on Facebook two weeks after the shooting of Michael Brown and published a pointed article by Rebecca Carroll, columnist of the Guardian demanding more protests from white Americans on the subject of racism.
"I hope that women who have a different but parallel reason for understanding an inherent danger to the bodies - and opinions on the race, which are markedly different in public polls - will cite the changes," Steinem wrote.
Although Steinem stands for a rather intersectional feminism, in which racial as well as gender issues are addressed, some of her comments on the racial issue in America are baffled.
Christina Sommers, a researcher at the American Enterprise Institute and author of "Who Stole Feminism?" told She The People that young men in the United States, especially young colored men, "are far more vulnerable than their sisters," but Steinem's remarks on Ferguson stand in contrast to their earlier criticism.
There are now hundreds of special programs for girls and young women, but almost nothing for boys.
But when the White House recently launched a small program, My Brother's Keeper, to help vulnerable young black and Hispanic men, there have been annoying reactions from many feminists, including Gloria Steinem.
The relationship of the police to the black community is not the only problem that pushes itself to the foreground of mainstream feminism.
After the U.S. border police picked up nearly 63,000 unaccompanied minors on the country's south-west border this year, the issue of immigration reform is also coming to the surface again - this time as a women's issue.
Andrea Mercado, co-chairwoman of We Belong Together, an organization that mobilizes women for immigration reform, said that for re-exampling the issue as an essential women's theme she only needs to publish immigrant women's stories.
"These speak for themselves," Mercado said.
When the vast majority of work visas go to male immigrants, the women who accompany them are in a position where they are vulnerable to domestic violence and exploitation.
"These stories find a reverberation in the women's organizations," she said.
"Pig House" in Ipswich is for sale
The image of a house for sale was removed by real estate agents when it became clear it showed a large pig that had made itself comfortable in the living room.
The detached house has one bedroom, located on a private street in Ipswich and is offered for £120,000.
Among the details was an illustration of the house's living room - which also included a pig that had bedded its head on a sofa.
Real estate agent Connells said the pig in the photo is the owner's pet.
"However, the photo was published erroneously and has since been removed," a spokeswoman for the company said.
In the exposé of the house, Connells described the former meeting house as a "unique one-family one-bedred house."
Even thince the broker has removed the image from the website, it still appears on advertising material.
House sales caused interest on social media when some asked if the pig was included in the purchase price.
The SW city Harvey is home to a new millionaire after winning the lottery
There's a lottery player from Harvey this month's turn
The search for a new millionaire in the south-west of Perth continues, with a lucky winner getting nearly $1.1 million from Saturday's lottery draw.
For the second time in five years, a newsstand in Harvey drew the jackpot in the first-division draw, down from $1 million in November 2009.
However, the lucky winner has yet to come forward.
Shopkeeper Steve Forward said the gain provides conversation in the small town in south-western Perth.
The city isn't talking about anything else, and everyone's pretty excited.
We thought such a profit could occur.
Eight WA winners celebrated winnings in the first division last month, a due conclusion for a few happy weeks for players in the state.
Last week, a Canning Vale player became one of the five millionaires in August after a Belmont-based couple made the mega profit of $7.5-million just a few weeks earlier.
The winning couple had been playing for twenty years and said the prize would allow them the possibility of a trip around the world and buying a new house.
The millionaire from Harvey is the 59th winner of the first division in WA this year, with a total of nearly $85 million.
Leonidas asked into gladiatorial arena
Not only the gladiators stood ankle-deep in the mud at the first Rheinbacher Römertag in the theme park.
But the appeal of the event was strong enough to attract well over 1000 visitors yesterday, who defied the rain showers and the autumnal cold and were completely inspired by the historical spectacle.
There was a colorful series of representations, performances, games, exhibits and information about the time of the Romans and their importance for the Rhineland.
The main attraction of the day was undoubtedly the martial fighter troupe from the gladiatorial school "Amor Mortis", which used every rain break to demonstrate its robust martial art in the arena prepared with soft sand.
Gladiator owner "Leonidas" proved to be a humorous and knowledgeable presenter and successively sent his men into the ring to the heroic duel in various gladiatorial genres.
At first it was the "provocators" in turn with a rectangular shield and short sword as well as leg splint on the left leg and arm rail on the right arm.
This was followed by the "Essedarii" with chest shield, short sword and litter flance as well as iron gladiator's helmet, leg and arm protection.
Particularly impressive was the appearance of "Retiarius", who competed against the "Secutor" with throwing net, trident and dagger and pushed the boys to the ground after a short fight.
Then the swords crashed metallically against each other, the shields scrambled and the throwers flew whirring through the arena that you could be afraid and afraid about the health of the gladiators.
But the imposing "Summa Rudis", the muscular referee, always had an attentive eye.
"We wanted to revive Roman everyday life and make the culture of the Roman period tangible," explained Lorenz Euskirchen, chairman of the organizer "Freundeskreis Römerkanal", the intention behind the spectacle.
After two years of preparation, the amusement park became an "Oppidum Romanum", a Roman city for one day, as Vice Mayor Claus Wehage stated in his greeting.
After all, the Romans have brought not only war and conquest in our region, but also Roman culture and numerous buildings that we still admire today.
Remnants of this could also be admired at Roman Day, such as "Aquedukt marble" from the eleven-meter-hight and 1400-meter-long Römerkanal bridge, which once stood near Lüftelberg.
The Frontinus-Society confronted the water and energy supply once and now, and Managing Director Petra Fricke knew: "We are still benefiting from the knowledge of back then".
This is also confirmed by graduate surveying engineer Ralph Heiliger from "IngenieurteamZwei" from Rheinbach, who presented surveying instruments of the Romans such as Chorobat, Groma or Dioptra.
The Römervilla in Blankenheim presented sketches and models from the excavations in 1894 as well as bricks from the original mortgage, the warm-air heating that once warmed the Roman villa.
Impressive also the footprints that had been left by wolf and fox two millennia ago in the footsteps of clay before burning them.
In 2011, the Office for Soil Monument Care had excavated the remains of a Roman villa in the Gewerbepark Nord in Rheinbach.
They had found perimeter walls of a farm building with a darre for drying grain and malting.
The building probably belonged to Villa Rustica, an agricultural estate, explained the archaeologist Dr. Jennifer Morscheiser.
A series of finds from these excavations she presented in a glass display case, including a large plate, several fine glass jars and parts of a dainty necklace.
The villa probably existed from the first to the fourth century AD, until the incursion of the Germans in the then Roman Rhineland, Morscheiser suspects.
With her, the children also dealt with Roman windmill play, similar to today's mill game, or they, like the Roman pupils once, wrote their names with metal handles on wax panels.
At the stand of the Römerthermen Zülpich, the children were allowed to lay mosaics themselves, tinker glass mosaics at the booth of the Rheinbach Glasmosaiken and operate a heavy wheat mill made of stone at the stand of the Eifel Nettersheim nature centre in order to grindle the flour for a flatbread or cereal.
With the faithfully reconstructed Roman touring van drawn by two cold-blooders, the visitors drove through the amusement park and also passed by the impressive stand of the Städtische Gymnasium, which presented the results of their "Roman project weeks" here.
With a Roman week from 13 to 21 September, the municipality of Wachtberg will also contribute to the Roman year 2014, which was proclaimed throughout Rhineland, which is supposed to commemorate the death of Emperor Augustus 2000 years ago.
Together with the home and history associations as well as the Wachtberger Wander-Verein, the Partnerschaftsverein and the Laienspielkreis Oberbachem, among other things, thematic hikes, a lecture, cooking evenings and guided tours for school classes will be offered and commemorating the Roman past of Wachtberg.
For example, a Roman legionnaire is told about his work on the draughty Wachtberg, plants are presented as living monuments from the Roman period and a funny lesson in terms of international understanding between a group of Wachtberger "Germans" and admitted Romans.
As part of a project week, Adendorfer Elementary School devotes itself to the Roman theme.
With the Römerwoche, the numerous traces of the Romans in Wachtberg, which are no longer visible at first glance, are to be experienced again.
In the Wachtberger Rathaus, a small presentation will commemorate the discovery of the Berkum matron sanctuary in 1879.
Ebola: Epidemic puts a strain on West Africa's economy
The Ebola epidemic in West Africa is also a major blow to the economy.
Markets are orphaned - and international companies are at a distance.
The devastating Ebola epidemic deals a severe blow to West Africa's economy.
The harvest rots in the fields, the mines are abandoned and the markets orphaned - the virus has severely affected the region.
Some international companies are also already at a distance.
"The Ebola epidemic is not only a medical crisis, but also an economic crisis," the head of the African Development Bank, Donald Kaberuka, said in a statement.
Liberia is the hardest affected
"It's a total disaster, we're losing a lot of money," says Alhaji Bamogo, who sells clothes at Liberia's second-largest market in Liberia's capital Monrovia.
Only those who want to buy food or something for disinfection against Ebola come on the market.
Liberia is the hardest hit by the virus, here alone more than 600 people fell victim to the fever.
Recently, the health authorities declared that the disease had penetrated into every corner of the country.
The virus has also spread in Guinea, Sierra Leone and Nigeria.
In total, more than 1500 people have died, with thousands more infected.
Foreign companies withdraw employees
Anywhere in the resource-rich countries, companies are suspending their operations.
Foreign companies such as the steel group Arcelor Mittal are withdrawing their employees, several international airlines no longer fly to the affected regions.
The local traders and farmers are also reacting: only a few merchants still leave their hometowns to get supplies.
In some areas in Sierra Leone and Liberia, which are quarantined, among other things, the cocoa and coffee beans that are important for exporture in the fields are rotting.
Farmers don't dare to leave home for the harvest.
Groceries are becoming scarce
"People are going to travel fewer and fewer," says Phillipe De Vreyer, Parisian expert on West Africa's economy.
The man, for example, who usually goes to the market and sells his vegetables, prefers to stay at home.
The population is therefore feeling the consequences of the epidemic directly and urgently: food is becoming scarce.
In the markets, the supply of staples such as rice is dwindling.
At the same time, the region, which is heavily dependent on international companies, threatens image damage - with far-reaching consequences.
The greatest danger is that international companies basically avoid the area for a long time, says Philippe Hugon of the French think tank Iris.
The decisive factor is how far and how long the virus will spread.
"Investors may have the impression that it is dangerous to live in Guinea, Sierra Leone and Liberia and thus also to invest," warns Hugon.
The disease is also putting a strain on state budgets on
The disease is also putting a strain on the already strained state budgets of the affected states.
The rating agency Moody's fears that their budgets will be severely strained by high spending on the health care system and additionally by the deteriorating economy.
The African Development Bank already promised to help the health systems of the affected countries with 60 million dollars (45 million euros).
The effects are already noticeable in Nigeria, where only a few Ebola cases have been diagnosed so far.
Although the oil-rich region in the southern Niger Delta has not yet been affected.
But elsewhere, many people already stay at home for fear of contagion.
Reservations at hotels are also declining significantly.
Shooters celebrate their 50th anniversary
The Schützenverein "Tannenwald" Berngau celebrates its 50-year-old founding festival from 12 to 14 September.
Preparations for the 50-year-old foundation festival from 12 to 14 September will be in full swing at the Schützenverein Tannenwald.
On Friday, September 12, a big entertainment evening with the group "Dorfrocker" is planned.
On Saturday at 6 p.m. it's the big boll shooting.
Afterwards in the big marquee is the mood evening with the "Stoapfälzer Spitzbuam".
Blaskapelle takes care of the music
Highlights on Sunday are the festive service and 9.30 a.m. in the parish church and the big pageant at 2 p.m.
In the afternoon is also the price distribution of federal and gau shooting.
A particularly beautiful sight will be the 25 party ladies with their dirndls.
Festkapelle is the brass band Berngau.
Mayor Wolfgang Wild and State Secretary Albert Füracker have taken over the patronage.
The original designation "Schützengesellschaft Tannenwald Berngau" dates from 1907, when on 21 March the association for "social entertainment was founded by means of an exercise in room socket and small caliber shooting".
The Nazi regime had prompted the dissolution of the association in 1937.
It was not until 1964 that 19 young men found each other again, who launched the "new" Schützenverein "Tannenwald" Berngau on April 30 in the Gasthaus Lukas.
Karl March and Adolf Ried led the club through a difficult starting time as shooting champions, in which it was primarily about finding a club restaurant and installing the shooting operation.
Already in the autumn of 1965, the shooting club started in the inn "Leininger" with two teams in the lap competitions.
In the following years, the sports shooters, who were now organized into seven teams, established themselves in all classes up to the 1st Gauliga.
The club's records tumbled every year and the magical limit of 380 rings was reached for the first time in 1980 by Rudi Meier.
Just as the performance of active shooters has increased, the number of members has also developed positively.
After the "Leininger" club restaurant no longer met the requirements of a modern shooting club, the then board under the leadership of Johann Rinn decided to create a future domicile in the basement rooms of the new "Landgasthof Härteis".
After a work of 2700 hours on May 8, 1988, Pastor Strasser granted the church blessing for the new shooting rooms.
One of the great highlights in the club's history was certainly the 44th gau shooting, combined with the solemn consecration of the new shooting flag in July 1991.
On 23 and 24 April 1994, the Schützenverein celebrated its 30th anniversary.
In 1995, a boulder group was founded.
Josef Schmalzl became firecracker speaker.
A great challenge for the "Böllerer" and for the club as a whole was the orientation of the Bavarian Böllertreffen 2005.
In many hours of voluntary work, the members of the association modernized the rooms and the shooting range in the Berngauer Hof last year and thus brought them up to date.
Eleven teams in lap competitions
You can also see the sporting achievements of the shooting club.
A total of eleven teams are currently taking part in the lap competitions: six aerial rifle teams, an air gun squad, a sports gun KK1, one youth and two AH teams.
The 1st team with Lars Ellrich, Katharina Ellrich, Steffi Grad and Kerstin Kellermann has ascended from the Gauoberliga (highest league in Schützengau ANB) to the Bezirksliga 1 in the round 2013/14.
Team 2 with Verena Moosburger Alexandra Meier, Eva Schuhmann and Tobias Graml has ascended from Gauliga Nord to the Gauoberliga.
In addition to shooting sports, events such as buck sting, carnival and king's ball, club outing and Christmas party have tradition.
The shooters are also present at the festivals in the parish and community.
Tony Stewart suffers accident on return to the racetrack
Tony Stewart's return to the racetrack ended about halfway through his race at the Atlanta Motor Speedway.
Stewart raced against the wall for the second time after his right front tyre burst on lap 172 and ended his evening.
Stewart drove his damaged car into the garage and left it without talking to journalists.
His team principal Chad Johnston said his 14-strong team is disappointed but is now focused on next weekend's race in Richmond, Virginia.
"I wish we'd had a better effort and a better result for him," Johnston said.
We're going to Richmond hoping we can do better there.
Stewart drove into a wall earlier at Sunday night's race after a collision with Kyle Busch and had to have the right side of his car repaired.
"I've gone into a good result today with pretty good hopes," Johnston said, adding: "It just didn't work out."
Not long after the second accident, Stewart's car was loaded into the tug and the team had packed and was ready for departure.
Stewart dropped three NASCAR sprint cup races after his car hit and killed another driver at an ash track race in northern New York state.
He decided to return this week and win either in Atlanta or at the next race in Richmond to win a place in the pursuit.
He returned to his workplace during an investigation into the tragic incident that led to the death of 20-year-old Kevin Ward Jr. This one had stepped onto the carriageway during a race to confront Stewart.
Authorities said on Friday that investigations into the cause of the accident will continue for at least another two weeks.
No decision has yet been made as to whether Stewart will face an indictment.
The three-time champion was received with great acclaim when he was introduced before the race.
After a start to 12th position, Stewart had already advanced into the first 10 shortly afterwards and was temporarily in fourth place.
After a restart in lap 122, Buschs smashed
Carriage with the no. 18 out of control after the exit from curve 2 and collided with Stewart, with both carriages driving against the wall.
Stewart drove on but dropped back to #21.
Dance-loving offspring wanted
Just try it: Laura, Lena, Lisa, Marie, Bettina, Emma and director Lisa Neitzel (from left) are looking forward to comrades-in-arms.
Like many other clubs, the jazz groups on TV Jahn have to create all-day lessons at secondary schools.
After the summer holidays, some groups decimated sharply.
Now we are increasingly looking for a sport that is healthy and great fun.
In the two jazz and modern-dance groups by Lisa Neitzel, former dancer in the jazz and modern dance formation "Spirits", there are only nine children left.
"Three in the group of eight- to twelve-year-olds, six in the group of twelve- to 15-year-olds," the 20-year-old recounts.
With more active people, the whole thing would be significantly more fun, "you could dance completely different choreographies," she says.
If you fancy dancing, you can sniff into the training session on Thursdays between 5 p.m. and 6 p.m. or on Fridays between 5 p.m. and 6:30 p.m. in the dance sports room at the former Hotel Meyer, Freistraße 9.
"The training starts regularly with a warm-up program," says the trainer.
Thereafter, various choreographies are practiced.
JazzDance has the trait that there is no set shape and is therefore open to many styles.
Elements from folklore to classical music to sports and acrobatics are processed in jazz dance.
Of course, sociability is not neglected either.
"We also go for dinner, watch dance movies and we've already stayed in the dance room," reports Lisa Neitzel.
In the group you can make friendships, share a beautiful hobby and do a lot for your own motor development.
If jazz dance is not like that, you can also sniff into one of the other offers of the TV Jahn.
Children and adolescents can sniff into class every Wednesday in children's dancing/dancing, bambini ballet, video clip dancing and hip-hop.
The dance coach is Francisco Afonso.
Adult couples are welcome to the dance evenings on Monday and Wednesday.
If you are interested in Discofox, Slow Waltz, Viennese Waltz, Rumba, Cha-Cha-Cha and Tango, you can stop by and participate.
Bulgarian prison supervisors protest nationwide
Hundreds of imprisoners from all over Bulgaria have organized a nationwide protest in front of the Ministry of Justice in the capital Sofia.
In the peaceful demonstration, they reiterated their demands for restoration of their old working conditions, which have recently been amended.
Higher salaries are also on a demand list, which is to be discussed with interim Justice Minister Hristo Ivanov.
Officials have been symbolically protesting against the new working hours for a month, calling for a return to the earlier 24-hour shifts.
Despite meetings between the union of prison employees and representatives of the Ministry of Justice, no agreement has been reached, the national Bulgarian ratio (BNR) reported.
Negotiations are ongoing, the director-general for implementing punitive measures, Rosen Zhelyazkov told the BNR.
The protest of the prison staff is expected to be supported by members of the Union Association of Employees of the Ministry of the Interior.
Ukrainian army has abandoned Luhansk airport
In eastern Ukraine, pro-Russian fighters are still on the rise.
After heavy battles, in which Russian tank battalions were allegedly also involved, the Ukrainian army gave up the airport of the rebel stronghold of Luhansk today, Monday.
At the same time, the army said that seven soldiers had been killed in the past 24 hours.
Ukrainian President Petro Poroshenko had previously drawn personnel consequences from his army's heavy defeats in the fight against the separatists and announced changes in the top army leadership, without giving details.
Pro-Russian separatists shot down plane
In the fight against Ukrainian government forces, pro-Russian separatists, according to their own account, once again shot down a warplane and two military helicopters.
About 100 soldiers were also wounded or killed in battles in the Donetsk region, the insurgents said on Monday, according to Russian agencies.
Coast guard ships sunken
The insurgents also claimed to have sunk two boats of the Ukrainian coast guard for the first time in the Sea of Azov.
Ukraine's border guard confirmed that two of its ships had come undercaded Sunday near the port city of Mariupol.
Seven border guards were wounded, a spokesman in Kiev told 112.ua.
Two crew members were considered missing.
Local media blamed Russia for the incident.
The separatists also reported that they had brought several localities in the Donetsk area under their control.
Diplomatic Offensive in Minsk
In the Belarusian capital Minsk, the Ukraine contact group is meeting today.
In addition to representatives of Ukraine, Russia and the OSCE, this should also be attended by an envoy of the separatists from eastern Ukraine.
In advance, all actors used their performances to publicly announce their demand.
NATO won't break agreements with Russia
NATO will not break its agreement with Russia as a result of the planned deployments in Eastern Europe as a result of the Ukraine crisis.
NATO Secretary-General Anders Fogh Rasmussen said in Brussels on Monday that NATO adheres to the founding agreement of the NATO-Russia Council of 1997.
The NATO chief, however, accused Russia "of blatant breach" of the agreement.
"Today, we have to face the reality that Russia does not regard NATO as a partner," Rasmussen said.
Documents and statements from the Russian side state that "they regard us as opponents".
Stronger NATO presence
Rasmussen has also announced plans to step up NATO's presence in Eastern Europe.
A "readiness action plan" to be adopted at the NATO summit in Wales will ensure that "we will have more visible NATO presence in the east as long as this is necessary," Rasmussen said in Brussels on Monday.
Kiev and Moscow agree on new gas talks
Russia and Ukraine have agreed on new talks to resolve their gas dispute, according to official figures.
Negotiations are scheduled for next Saturday, Russian Energy Minister Alexander Nowak said Monday.
It is still waiting for a decision of the European Union whether it will also send a representative to the talks.
Due to the dispute over the price of gas, Ukraine hasn't been getting fuel from Russia for weeks.
Most recently, EU Energy Commissioner Günther Oettinger had mediated between the two sides.
Ukraine had warned Russia could also disrupt gas supplies for Europe.
Europe draws about a third of its natural gas from Russian sources, of which a large part flows through Ukraine.
Merkel speaks power word: Toll will come 'very clear'
Now the chancellor is once again clearly committed to it.
It’s not a free pass for all complicated details, however.
Persistent concerns from the CDU against the passenger car toll have the CSU grieved increasingly.
German chancellor Angela Merkel has spoken a power word for the introduction of a passenger car toll after ongoing resistance from her CDU.
"To put it very clearly: she is in the coalition agreement, and she will come," the party leader said on Monday after meetings of the CDU's top bodies in Berlin.
Merkel, however, did not decide on a detailed model for the time being.
In the black-red government program, "certain parameters" are mentioned.
Federal Transport Minister Alexander Dobrindt (CSU) has "made a proposal, and this is now being worked on."
The NRW-CDU, however, insists on its no to a toll on all roads.
Merkel emphasized that Finance Minister Wolfgang Schäuble (CDU) is also involved constructively in the deliberations for a bill.
In addition to the topic, he had also spoken with Dobrindt about further possibilities to activate more private capital for investments in infrastructure, for example in broadband expansion.
Schäuble had left a "mirror" report uncommented on Sunday, his house examining its own concept to remunerate highway investors with toll revenue - after the 2017 election.
CSU leader Horst Seehofer then warned against developing toll concepts against the leading minister in the cabinet.
CDU federal deputy Armin Laschet reaffirmed his objections.
"The coalition agreement never assumed to mowing all county and federal roads," the North Rhine-Westphalian CDU state chairman said.
In this respect, it is an important signal that Seehofer has now announced to talk about the topic again.
Resistance to the plans also comes from the CDU state associations Baden-Württemberg and Rhineland-Palatinate.
The background is fears that a toll affects the small border traffic to neighbouring states.
Seehofer had demanded coalition fidelity because of the toll criticism from ranks of the CDU.
The left sees the plans as a failure in view of the dispute in the Union.
"The Murks toll is dead," party leader Bernd Riexinger told the Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung (Monday).
Dobrindt wants to introduce a vignette requirement on all German roads, which is scheduled to start in 2016.
Domestic car owners may not be additionally burdened according to the coalition agreement.
Therefore, they should receive a full compensation for the toll via the motor vehicle tax.
According to EU law, however, the model may not disadvantage foreign drivers because of their nationality.
Genetic Disorder Often Misdiagnosed
One British woman says as a result of a misdiagnosis for many years that she would die.
Karin Rodgers lived almost her entire adolescence in the belief that she had only a few more years to live after being diagnosed with another disease.
She actually suffered from Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease (CMT) - a group of inherited disorders that damage the nerves outside the brain and spinal cord.
Charity CMT UK shared that misdiagnosis is a common problem in people with CMT because so little is known about this disease.
About 23,000 people in the UK are believed to be suffering from CMT, which can lead to motor disorders such as musculos weakness, an unsafe gait and curved toes.
Sufferers also suffer from numbness or pain. The disease is incurable and progressive, which means the symptoms get worse over time.
When Rodgers was 13, she believed she was suffering from Friedreich-Ataxie (FA) - a disease with very poor chances of survivance.
Rodgers thought she'd sit in a wheelchair before she turned 18, and die before her 30th birthday.
The mother of two, who is now 51, said: "As a child, I knew I couldn't do the same as the others.
I was constantly falling and everything lasted longer with me.
I could never ride a roller skate or skateboard with the other children and was often teased at school because of my way of walking and running.
Rodgers said that at the age of 13, after several surgeries to relieve her Achilles tendons and stretching her feet, she saw her medical record when her doctor had left the room and read that she suffered from FA.
"I felt guilty because I had been curious, so I didn't tell this to anyone, but I went home, searched for the illness in the library and wrote to the FA society," she said.
When I received an answer, I was shocked and drearly panicked.
I thought I'd be in a wheelchair at the age of 18 and die at 25, and in between I'll slowly lose all my skills.
I made it all out with myself and planned my own funeral.
She said when she turned 17, she realized that her abilities hadn't diminished as much as she had imagined and questioned her doctor.
He just got up and hugged me and said, "My dear, I don't think you have that, otherwise you'd already be in a wheelchair right now."
I think you've got a little less life-threatening.
After a few genetic tests, CMT was detected in her.
"When he explained to me what CMT was, I felt like I was a winner," she said.
Charity CMT UK has launched a CMT awareness month to draw attention to this disease.
Staff sergeant Berni Diether has been working as a fireworker since the closure of the former troop training site
For almost ten years, there has been no more shooting at the former military training ground in Münsingen.
Nevertheless, deadly ammunition still appears every month in the heart of the Swabian Alb biosphere area.
The grenades are sometimes so dangerous that they have to be blown up on the ground.
A deafening bang echoes over the heart of the biosphere area during the blasts.
In December 2004, the last sharp shot fell at the Münsingen military training ground, which was closed at the end of 2005 after 110 years of military operations.
Since this time, the 6500-hectare site has been the centerpiece of the Swabian Alb biosphere area, which is open to hikers and cyclists on 13 designated trails.
Talk burden of businessmen in the Washington area rises with economic recovery
A recovery of the economy means more customers, more sales and new jobs.
She also means higher taxes.
State and local tax assessments for companies in the country rose slightly last year, while economic recovery accelerated, according to a newly published study last week, and firms in the Washington area were no exception.
Companies in the District, Maryland and Virginia area jointly paid $27.6 billion to government and local entities in the 2013 financial year, a 3.8 per cent increase over 2012 revenues amounting to $26.6 billion.
Companies' government and local tax burden rose 4.3 percent to $671 billion last year, compared to 3.9 percent the year before, and it was the third year of growth in a roward after the multi-year declining notices of 2009 and 2010.
State tax rose more sharply than local levies, by 4.3 percent, according to the study conducted by service agency Ernst & Young with the Center for State Taxation, a tax policy group.
More than half of the district's tax revenue, 56 percent, comes from corporate taxes, while 36 percent of Maryland's revenue comes from companies.
Virginia generates the lowest tax revenue from corporations at 28 percent.
Much of the increase in tax revenues is due to a recovery in the companies' real estate values, the researchers say, which led to a 3.7 percent increase in property taxes this year after the three consecutive years with growth rates of less than 1 percent.
While much of these gains came from major states such as California, New York and Texas, the process also appears to be true for the Washington area.
Companies in the District, Maryland and Virginia jointly paid over $10 billion in government and local property taxes last year, compared to $9.6 billion in 2012 - a 4.2 percent increase over the course of a year.
But the apparent recovery in real estate values is not so effective in raising government and local tax revenue in Maryland, as property taxes here amount to just over one-fifth of companies' task notices.
Companies in Virginia and D.C. pay nearly half of their state and local taxes in the form of property taxes.
"In Maryland, a large portion of the real estate, particularly in the tax area around D.C., is either owned by the government or by non-profit organizations that don't pay property taxes," said Douglas Lindholm, managing director of the Center on State Taxation.
Maryland is therefore forced, for example, to rely much more on income taxes in order to provide the same services offered by other states.
The recent recovery in tax revenues by companies cannot be attributed solely to the recovery of the real estate market
Corporate revenue also appears to be recovering, according to the data.
Companies in the region reported income taxes at state organizations of $2.3billion compared to 2.1 billion in 2012.
Maryland took $1 billion into corporate income taxes, the region's highest amount.
While the trend is heading in the same direction, the local tax burden on businesses is not rising evenly in all three places.
The increase rate in the district corresponded to the national average at 4.3 percent, while the increase in Maryland was significantly higher, 4.9 percent.
Only Virginia reported below-average tax revenue of 4.1 percent.
With 6 percent in all three areas of office, Virginia has the significantly lowest corporate income tax (in all three there are fixed corporate tax rates).
The corporate tax rate in Maryland is 8.25 per cent, and in the district relatively high 9.975 per cent.
A comparable study by the U.S. Chamber of Commerce earlier this year showed that lower government and local corporate taxes are required in Virginia and there is a better fiscal climate overall than in Maryland.
In the study, the district was not studied.
Despite the benefits, Virginia is under pressure from its southern neighbor.
Northo Carolina has passed legislation to lower its corporate tax rates this year from 6.9 percent to 6 percent, the same rate as Virginia, and the rate will fall to 5 percent in the coming year.
If the state continues to meet its income targets in the coming year, this law could reduce the state's corporate income tax rate to 3 percent by 2017.
In the meantime, the small businesses in the Washington area seem to be doing better, and they therefore pay more government and local taxes, according to the latest study.
Corporate taxes paid as personal income taxes by small businessmen - a transitional structure according to which most small businesses are organized - in the District, Maryland and Virginia rose 20 percent to $2.4 billion last year, a significantly faster growth rate than corporate taxes overall.
The researchers say the trend and increase in corporate taxes across the country is likely to continue this year, with an 6.2 per cent increase in general multi-defense tax revenues for the state and local governments in the first three quarters of 2014 compared to the same period last year.
Still, the Washington area could lag behind if the federal government's spending cut affects the labor market in the region.
Partner city: German beer for Guangzhou
Hops and malt - also a popular combination in China.
German beer enjoys an excellent reputation in China.
Frankfurt's partner city in China now wants a brewery and also hopes for a direct flight connection between the two cities.
German beer brewed in Frankfurt's partner city Guangzhou - that's the urgent request of the officials in China.
"We would be very happy if a German brewery would settle down in our city," Li Yiwei's deputy party secretary of the southern Chinese metropolitanism said this week while visiting an SPD delegation in his city.
Time and again, the functionary of China's ruling Communist Party came back to this concern of the Twelve-Million metropolis during the course of the conversation.
Guangzhou, which in Germany is also known as the canton, is one of China's richest cities, Li advertised.
That’s why people also have the purchasing power to afford German beer.
Hessian SPD chairman Thorsten Schäfer-Gümbel, who led the German delegation of politicians, told the host that he would look for a possible partner for the project in Germany.
"It doesn't take much for beer: good water, grain and joie de vivre," he added at lunch with the party superior in Guangzhou.
This joie de vivre already radiated Mr. Li, he flattered the host.
As a result, the functionary from Guangzhou tossed with shepherd-Gümbel - but with red wine, which was served for the food.
The Chinese brand Tsingtao goes back to German brewing art.
Tsingtao beer is brewed according to Germany's purity bid.
More business transactions
A second wish of the Chinese hosts would be a direct flight connection between Frankfurt and Guangzhou, deputy party leader Li said.
Lufthansa had offered the flight earlier, but cancelled it for economic reasons in 2012.
"It would be worthwhile again," the local politician was convinced.
"Business transactions between our two cities are increasing."
Currently, travelers would have to take the detour via Changsha in Hunan province, Li complained.
The administration of Guangdong Province, whose capital is Guangzhou, is also hoping for the flight connection.
The deputy governor of the province, Chen Yunxian, put this wish in the foreground during his conversation with the Social Democrats.
"That's a very good idea," Schäfer-Gümbel replied - Chinese Deputy Minister Li Jinjun, an intimate connoisseur of Germany, nodded approvingly.
Nursed partnership
Guangzhou's ambiguous deputy party leader hopes he can visit Frankfurt next year for a partnership meeting.
Li Yiwei was already on Main, but not yet in this function.
He only came into office this year, after his predecessor had been promoted from office because of allegations of corruption.
Local party leader Wan Qinglian also had to leave.
The crackdown on Guangzhou officials is part of a nationwide campaign against corruption in the party and administration, which is enforced with hardship by head of state Xi Jinping.
The new top politician Li Yiwei had worked for an American company before his party career, which is highly unusual in the Chinese Communist Party.
Prior to taking office in Guangzhou, he served as a top official in Fujian, where carmaker VW built a large plant.
More than a dozen city partnerships have been officially entered into Frankfurt.
The connection to Guangzhou, 9000 kilometers away, is one of those maintained from both sides, for example with a student exchange.
Last year, the 25-year anniversary of the German-Chinese connection was celebrated.
The southern Chinese metropolis on the Pearl River is almost as strong as all of Hesse with its economic power.
Next year, it wants to reach a gross domestic product of 250 billion euros, Li reported.
