Amazon buys Twitch for $1.04 billion
AMAZON has just confirmed what information had reported on Monday morning: The online retailer - and video producer, and a variety of other things - is buying video streaming service Twitch for $1.04bn (US$970 million).
The announcement comes as a surprise, not because no one was expecting a takeover of Twitch, but because it was widely suspected that YouTube would acquire the company.
Three months ago, it looked as if selling Twitch to Google's video service for a cool billion-decided thing, and the co-operation also seemed natural.
Twitch, founded only three years ago as Justin.tv, was launched as a general streaming service - a live version of YouTube.
Instead, it quickly became a platform for players who wanted to release their bravura pieces in games, a "YouTube for live games," according to an insider.
The Twitch interface.
And "let's play," a video genre in which know-it-alls give the (mostly older) games a coat of sanctuarial science theatre, are already very popular on YouTube.
It’s just that YouTube is often mentioned when you want to describe Twitch, so the news that YouTube Twitch wanted to buy was recorded a bit bored, a textbook example of an established technology company that buys up a potential competitor.
It's a puzzle of why the deal with YouTube didn't materialize, since the company was reportedly willing to pay as much as Amazon.
All we know about it at the moment is this statement from Emmet Shear, president of Twitch: "We opted for Amazon because they believe in our community, they share our values and long-term visions and they want to help us get there faster."
Another secret, frankly, is Twitch’s incredible success.
For snobs like me, who explain that they'd rather play sports rather than watch, it's hard to comprehend the attraction, which assumes to watch others play instead of taking the control console into their own hands.
It's one thing to look over a friend's shoulder at 3 a.m. as she sneaks through Resident Evil, and something completely other, watching a weirdo shot 20 times after each other in Call of Duty.
You could watch all these games right now.
Another problem lies in the fact that many of the most popular games today are ego games, so watching without your own control over the viewing perspective can be a really good recipe for headaches.
I'll admit that the quick runs, in which the Roger Bannisters of our electronics age play entire games in record time, are entertaining.
But as long as there is not a virtuoso or humour, his Twitch channel is presumably not so interesting.
But what do skeptics like me already know about it?
Twitch is accessed by 55 million different visitors per month and is the fourth-largest source of Internet traffic spikes.
And how exactly does Amazon want to make money out of it?
It's hard to imagine Twitch being integrated as elegantly into Amazon Instant Video as YouTube Twitch just could have swallowed.
But Twitch has something every company would like: thousands of young men who are courted as advertisers.
As Twitch's chairman, Shear, said Amazon and Twitch "believe both of the future of gaming," and the medium shows no signs of shrinking, not even - swallow - as a spectator sport.
There was a lot going on on the bouncy castle at Burggartenfest on Saturday.
The concept of organizers was right.
Despite the heavy downpound on Saturday evening, an estimated 1200 music fans came to the open-air festival in the castle garden.
At times the entrance had to be stopped.
"That was annoying," said Ron Sem, one of the organizers.
Salzwedel l light and fun.
Maxim Sachraj and Ron Sem formed the name Amula from two Spanish words.
Because the open air in the Salzwedeler Burggarten should radiate brightness during the day and in the evening through various shows.
Well, and the fun comes by itself anyway.
This was already apparent early on Saturday, when the first visitors set off for open air.
The organizers had promised a celebration for all generations.
And they're able to do that.
Around the supply stalls on the Burggartenwiese, Salzwedeler, but also guests from the surrounding area had made themselves comfortable.
They followed the first live performances, bounced their foot, enjoyed cool drinks - in short: pure relaxation and enjoyment were trendy.
And this across the generations.
While some were slumbering on blankets on the extra carted white sand at the beach bar, others feasted on a quarter of wine in the vinotheque, the offspring raged on the bouncy castle, which was a great attraction for the smaller guests.
But also in children's make-up, in the truest sense of the word, the volunteers had their hands full in order to cope with the crowd and to conjure imaginative creations into the small faces.
The bigger ones took advantage of the kicker tables or participated in a skateboarding workshop, hosted by the Goodfoot crew from Salzwedel.
Until the big rain arrived.
Shortly before 5:30 p.m
But the attractiveness of the feast did not stop.
"There were more people than expected," said Ron Sem, who spoke of "success across the board."
That’s why there’s nothing opposed to a repeat of next year’s big party.
Although the content and size of the event has yet to be worked on, Sem made it clear in an initial assessment on Sunday compared to the people’s vote.
He described as annoyance that in the meantime the admission had to be stopped.
Obviously, the security company had been booked with too few forces.
As a result, some who had already paid no longer came to the site.
The response was very good.
In an initial cautious estimate, the Salzwedel co-organizer spoke of about 1200 visitors frolicking in the castle garden.
There were problems with sound technology.
The Salzwedel hip-hop band Block`n`Rox broke off their performance unnerved.
The technicians weren't able to create a semi-sensible sound.
The planned hookah lounges could not be set up because the tents could not be brought to Salzwedel because of a puncture.
"But next year they will be there," the organizer said.
The premiere of the Amula open-air was more than successful from the number of visitors.
And this in bad weather.
However, the organizers have made some mistakes, which will be forgiven the first time, but must be eliminated in the second edition.
The organizers also have to take criticism in the social network Facebook.
"Sorry, but for twelve euros you just expected more," Melanie Meier comments on the event page.
"Unfortunately, you took over a bit," says Ele Zimmermann in the same place.
Others don't see the initial problems quite so closely.
I think it's very good that some people not only talk stupidly, but also really try to put something together.
Hats off!" Stephanie Jenß writes to Amula.
Now the organizers have one year time to turn off these criticisms.
