Will the castle clubs be pulling together again soon?
Recently, there have been repeated disputes between the two Wolfratshauser Burgvereins.
Now the boards are thinking about possible cooperation.
Richard Dimbath, former chairman of Wolfratshauser Burgfreunde, cited the project in Guédelon in France as a model for the reconstruction of Wolfratshaus Castle.
Only a year ago there was no good climate between the Burgverein and Burgfreunde Wolfratshausen.
So far the points of view seemed very different, how to deal with the history of Wolfratshauser Burg and a possible reconstruction.
Now the two camps are moving towards each other: talks on a common approach are to be held soon.
This is confirmed by the club boards Torsten Sjöberg (Burgverein) and Herbert Piffl (Burgfreunde) at the request of our newspaper.
"I called to Mr Piffl and suggested that we could sit down once," reports Sjöberg.
In his view, all members of both clubs could pull together again in the future.
In this way, visible successes can be achieved more quickly.
While the castle friends around the suddenly deceased Richard Dimbath had a reconstruction of the castle on the mountain forest in mind, for the castle association around Sjöberg the "developing and experience" of the site is at the forefront.
Herbert Piffl, second chairman of the castle friends, was very happy about the call, according to his own information.
Since Dimbath's death, he's presided over the castle friends.
A new chairman is due to be found at a general meeting in mid-September.
According to the 74-year-old, the association currently has around 40 members.
"After the death of my friend Richard Dimbath, we initially rested the club's activities out of respect." This "thinking phase" was necessary "to "consider how it could go on."
According to Piffl, the new chairman should then go into the conversation with the castle club.
Until then, the castle friends could also take a current mood within their grouping.
There is currently no candidate for the presidency, says Piffl.
"But I'm confident that someone will find each other." He himself doesn't want to run because he's resident in Bad Tölz.
The new chairman should be a Wolfratshauser.
The Tölzer wanted to work on the project "but very happy to continue to cooperate".
The different viewpoints in terms of reconstruction are likely to remain in Piffl's eyes in the near future.
He pays tribute to Dimbath as a "missionary" who always believed in the reconstruction of the castle.
Two similar, well-advanced projects in Austria and France would show that this idea is not an aerial castle.
Due to a high participation of the population as well as through volunteers from all over the world, they are a tourist magnet.
Piffl: "Even I was skeptical at the beginning.
But over time, I've become more and more convinced that it makes sense to push ahead with the project." It could create a "unique selling point" for the rafting city.
There's nothing else here.
In addition, the project would be financeable through EU grants, visitor tickets and potential sponsors, Piffl believes.
"I don't want to throw the idea of the reconstruction overboard personally." He hopes that the Burgverein might move something on this point.
Their goal of first documenting history "is important and good."
After that, you could look further.
Clever ways to save on university textbooks
Since the cost of university textbooks for many students is more than $1,000 per year, near-two-semester student Matt Schroeder considered a clever savings idea.
He invented a lending system for books of higher-semester students and offered nominal compensation to delay the resale.
"My math book usually costs $180, but I got it for $10 for a semester and a Chick-fil-A cookie," said Schroeder, 19, who attends Covenant College in Georgia.
The books needed for the last semester would have cost him $430, he said.
He spent €120.
The college management said students, on average, spend more than $1,200 annually on textbooks and study material that there would be various cost mitigation options for the students, however.
Online shops and creative approaches such as the Schroeders are increasingly the solution of choice for students.
Renting textbooks is becoming more popular, and so students can use a book for one semester, often at a significantly cheaper price than when buying.
Neebo Inc, which operates more than 250 university book stores, said renting textbooks had doubled since 2011.
Surveys in the industry show that about a quarter of the books in the college bookstores were rented last spring semester, Neebo's vice-president Tevor Meyer said.
Less than half of all textbooks are purchased in campus bookstores, according to the National Association of College Stores.
Here's the one-on-one for the best deals.
Buy Online
Some new books cost only one-third of the price online for which they are available in the campus bookstore.
The ninth issuance of "Calculus" by Ron Larwon, Bruce Edwards and Robert Hostetler costs almost $290, but can be obtained from the specialized textbook retailer Chegg.com for $239.99.
Buy used
If you don’t mind the notes of other or normal signs of use, used books are a good option.
"Calculus" is needed on Chegg.com for $93.49.
Matt Casaday, 25, graduating student at Brigham Young University, says he spent 42 cents on Amazon.com for a used issuance of "Strategic Media Decisions: Understanding The Business End Of The Advertising Business."
The book was newly sold for $48.
Academics such as Ingrid Bracey, head of the University of Massachusetts at Amherst College Without Walls, advised students to inquire with their professors whether previous expenses were permitted.
Sometimes the updates are not relevant for the seminar papers.
In such cases, older expenses can often be purchased for a fraction of the cost of the latest issue.
In addition to Chegg and Amazon, Half.com from eBay Inc is also a popular alternative for used books.
Rent
Renting textbooks is worth considering if you treat them reasonably well, remember to return them, and don’t want to keep the books after completing the course.
You can savage more than 80 percent of the cost of a newly purchased book.
For example, a student could rent "Calculus" for about $20 for the duration of a semester.
Find out about the provisions of the store where you rent, including the penalty fees, which may arise for the writing of notes, underlinings or signs of use.
Tenant beware: If you don't return the book, the penalty fees may even exceed the price of a new book.
e-books
E-books instead of traditional textbooks are also an option.
Sometimes these books are de facto rented by providing an electronic license for a certain period of time from wholesalers such as Amazon.com, Chegg and Barnes & Noble Inc.
At Chegg you can rent "Calculus" for 6 months for about $61.
Bracey says that students in literature courses often find the best deals, as many classics can be downloaded for free nowadays, while scientific and engineering textbooks can be very expensive.
Definitely looking around anyway.
Joe Gault, 29, who will soon visit Pepperdine Law School, recommends the ISBN number for price determination on books, because you can be sure to find the right textbook.
And before an online order, one should check, according to Gault, whether the book is actually in stock.
He learned this lesson the hard way.
He bought a book that had four-week delivery time and eventually paid the full price in the college bookstore.
Why "Guardians of the Galaxy" didn't become a box-offer
"The Expendables 3" by Sylvester Stallone contributed less than 30 million dollars of its 90-million-dollar budget in the U.S., while "Sin City: A Dame to Kill For" pocketed just 12 million dollars in its 70-million-dollar budget.
The Cameron Diaz-tailored film "Sex Tape" grossed only 14.6 million dollars on the opening weekend, while children's films such as "Dragon's Tame Made Easy 2" also disappoint in revenue.
An article by telegraph film critic Robbie Collin hints that there could be the lack of quality in the films themselves.
Collin referred to as "the worst year" so far for children's films in 2014, describing "dragon taming easily made 2" as a purely "functional" fantasy sequence.
With films for a slightly older audience, however, it seems as if the quality of the films is not necessarily the problem.
In July, Entertainment Weehury compared the average CinemaScore and Metacritic rating for every summer movie launch, which was shown on at least 2000 screens between American Memorial Day and July 20 2013 and the current year.
The results are based on the opinion of moviegoers and film critics and indicate that this year's film hits are equivalent to those of the past year.
Some commentators believe that the development is due to changing viewing habits and the growing popularity of web streaming services such as Netflix.
Director Jon Favreau, who is currently working on Disney's soon-to-be-released Junglebuch film, told Hollywood Reporter: "I think times are changing."
We have to accept this and not try to follow the past.
In contrast to the currently weak cash results of Hollywood productions, Netflix recently generated revenues with streaming content amounting to 1.2 billion dollars, almost twice as much as last year, in which sales amounted to 837 million dollars.
Last summer, director Steven Spielberg criticized the movie studios for focusing too much on film adaptations of comics and predicted a "core meltdown" of Hollywood.
Seen in the light of the current downturn, some commentators argue that his bleak prophecy may well contain a true core.
However, due to the potential box-offices expected in the summer of 2015, including Avengers: Age of Ultron, Minions and Jurassic World, other representatives of the film industry are more optimistic:
X-Men's producer, Simon Kinberg, described the decline in cash results as simply "cyclical" and told Hollywood Reporter: "Next summer will be the biggest summer at the box office ever, and no one will worry about business anymore."
