The roughly 1000 protesters had been forced out of the PTV building by soldiers, Information Minister Pervaiz Rashid said.
The station has resumed the broadcast after about half an hour.
PTV director Athar Farooq said protesters equipped with sticks had damaged devices in the editorial board.
Dead or injured were not reported.
Protesters are demanding the resignation of the head of government, whom they accuse of electoral fraud.
Police said around a kilometre from Prime Minister Nawaz Sharif's residence, around 3000 protesters with security forces clashes resumed on Monday.
According to the hospital, a police officer was injured.
Over the weekend, at least three people had been killed and more than 500 were injured as demonstrators tried to advance to Sharif's residence.
They are calling for the resignation of the prime minister.
The powerful military had on Sunday evening, after a meeting at army headquarters, called for a political solution to the conflict "to waste without time and use it without violence."
“The army remains committed to ensuring the security of the state.”
Escalating violence has stoked concern about military intervention that has already cobbled more often in the past.
Protests led by opposition politician Imran Khan and preacher Tahirul Qadri have been ongoing since Independence Day on August 14.
Sharif opposes a resignation.
Khan and Qadri had called their supporters to persevere Sunday.
"We won't go back without forcing Sharif to resign," Khan said.
Qadri expressed himself similarly.
Khan accuses Sharif of winning the parliamentary election in May last year through fraud.
Qadri calls for the resignation of central and provincial governments.
Then a transitional government should reform the electoral system and proclaim new elections.
More than 40 000 security forces are deployed to protect the government district.
Demonstrators had initially peacefully persevered outside parliament until Khan and Qadri called on them over the weekend to advance to Sharif's residence.
