We showed a mighty trunk in our puzzle task of the summer series "Mein Stück vom Rhöner Glück" last Friday.
Several of these tree giants still exist in the district, the specimen pictured is the "Geissruheiche" above Unterweißenbrunn.
A bumpy footpath leads up to the 600-year-old oak tree.
It goes past paddocks, basalt stone heaps and basalt stone seas.
A bit below the quarry of Bauersberg, one kilometer north of the road to Weisbach, there she stands.
Vital looks the natural monument, it protrudes beyond the other trees of the environment and is reminiscent of an American sequoia with a circumference of more than eight meters.
A small Buddha on a tree stump indicates that it is a spiritual or mystical place.
But despite intensive research, there were only indications of the origin of the name.
Dr. Wolfwang Schneider specifically consulted the Bischofsheim archive and interviewed older people from Unterweißenbrunn to find out what kindness it has with the honey.
The result of his investigations remained the rather sober explanation for the name.
Until about 80 years ago, the goat herders are said to have driven their animals from Unterweißenbrunn onto the painstakingly cleared and stone-free meadows.
At the foot of the tree are said to have rested the animal keepers exhausted from the ascent.
In the meantime, the area has regained its importance, because on the meadows grew goat herds again.
The area has also been declared the core zone of the biosphere reserve, Schneider has further found out.
However, you should exercise caution when entering the stone accumulations, because the basalt warmed up by the sun is also a populous whereabouts for cruciferous otters.
A hiking trail leads from Unterweißenbrunn to the tree, which is somewhat hidden.
Other trails are signposted, climb up the direction of Hochrhön and invite you to an extended hike.
Where's the Wasserburg?
However, a water castle, which is searched for in our next task, is rather rarely found in height.
The reflection in the trench already gives a good impression of the building, but we have some historical buildings in the district, to which the building counterfeit could fit.
Where is our "piece of Rhöner Glück"?
My piece Rhöner Glück
As with previous series of Main-Post in previous years, there are prizes to be won again this year if they have the right solution.
If you like to photograph yourself, you can also participate.
Under the e-mail address red.neustadt@mainpost.de you can send us the solution and also your very personal "piece of Rhöner Glück" under this keyword.
The most beautiful photos will be published.
After weeks of tug-of-war, Italy's prime minister and incumbent EU chairman Matteo Renzi enforced the nomination of his Foreign Minister Federica Mogherini as head of EU foreign policy against strong Conservative opposition at the EU special summit last week.
Renzi considers, as the pro-government daily La Repubblica reproduces, the appointment of Mogherini, who is also regarded as a favorite in the government team, as strengthening the position of the Social Democrats and his own in the EU.
He also expects a boost for his "reform package" in Italy.
Last but not least, Renzi hopes to quiet the left-winning party base again and again against him, because Mogherini is assigned to him - albeit rather in a moderate way.
Donald Tusk, who was nominated as President-in-Office of the Council, with the Conservative representatives with Commission President Jean-Claude Juncker and Poland nominated as President-in-Office of the Council, will thus establish a political balance in Rome.
At the same time, the head diplomat of the EU will becommond Commission President Juncker.
The outstanding approval of the European Parliament is considered to be certain.
Otherwise, President Giorgio Napolitano would hardly have called the nomination of the 41-year-old politician of the ruling Democratic Party (PD) an "important recognition" of Italy's role in the EU.
La Repubblica spreads optimism that Mogherini, who has been proven for almost two decades in leading party offices and known for their ambition, will face the "enormous challenges".
The nominate is married and has two daughters.
Her party career began the graduate political scientist in 1996 in the youth organization of then Democratici di Sinistra (Left Democrats), which merged in 2008 with the Catholic center party Margherita to today's social-democratic PD.
Mogherini became vice-president of the European Community Organisation of Socialist Youth for three years.
With the 2001 election to the Left Democrat party council, she entered high party politics, worked in the foreign policy department, of which she soon became head.
She occupied traditional focal points of Italian foreign policy such as the Middle East, became well-known through studies on NATO deployment in Afghanistan, in which Italy is involved with a troop contingent, maintained contacts with the Social Democrats in Europe and with the Democrats in the USA.
Since 2008, she has been a member of the Chamber of Deputies, where she is an expert in defence and European and international affairs.
When Renzi became prime minister in February 2014, he summoned Mogherini to his secretary of state.
If the PD today again has a better relationship with the strong peace movement in the country, which contributed to the more than 40 percent votes of the party in the EU elections in May, Federica Mogherini has, as is noted in PD circles.
The reservations in Brussels that the new EU foreign envoy have too little diplomatic experience are therefore lacking in substance, is countered in the PD.
In Brussels, particularly by Poland and the Baltic members, mistruaciously realistic accents by Mogherini on the crisis in Ukraine are pursued and defamed as a "Russia-friendly course."
It endorses the South Stream pipeline operated by the Russian company Gasprom, through which Russian natural gas is to be transported directly to southern Europe by circumnavigation of Ukraine, which is also used to ensure Italy's energy security.
After Renzi terminated as EU chairman in July, Mogherini traveled to Moscow for her first state visit.
Photos showing them with Putin at shake-hands have been taken with outrage in Brussels, as well as that it invited the Kremlin chief to a Milan economic summit for October.
A total of nine young people started their training at emz-Hanauer in Nabburg on Monday.
Among them is an apprentice who has already completed another apprenticeship in the company.
In the future, the new employees will work in four training occupations: electronics technician (three trainees), mechatronics technician (two), tool mechanic (two) and process mechanic (two).
All apprentices come from Schwandorf: Tobias Braun and Thomas Bücherl from Schwarzach, Stefan Brickl and Florian Rewitzer from Schmidgaden, Johannes Beer from Neunburg vorm Wald, Felix Domaier from Oberviechtach, Daniel Sorgenfrei and Sebastian Zahner from Pfreimd as well as Ferdinand Strebl from Teunz.
The new apprentices previously attended primary or secondary schools and secondary schools.
From Wednesday to Friday, the trainees of all vintages as well as the instructors go to a team training event in Oberammergau, where, among other things, a full-day hike and rafting on the Inn are on the program.
The company has 435 employees throughout Germany and 950 employees worldwide.
The number of trainees spread over all vintages is 27.
Socialists in France should "keep their mouths shut and put France in order," Francois Hollande's main ally said.
Mr Valls called on the deeply divided leftist to show "their love" for the controversial socialist president, whose cabinet reshuffle has encountered disapproval among the vast majority of the French.
The president deserves everyone's respect, he deserves our loyalty, he deserves our support.
"It's our duty to stay by his side," he said on great applause.
As an appeasing gesture, the Prime Minister insisted that his government would not question the controversial 35-hour-week in France, despite fierce calls for an easing of the settlement by Emmanuel Macron, the new economy minister.
On Saturday, Mr Hollande had urged his co-socialists to show themselves "unified" with the government.
But Christiane Taubira, the justice minister, sprinkled sand into the gearge in which she appeared at a meeting of renegade socialist MPs and criticized the Socialist Party for depriving the French "the faith in its future."
Marine Le Pen, the far-right leader of the National Front, mocked the socialists' constant trench struggles and said she couldn't imagine that the new Vall's government would persevere for more than a few months.
Francois Hollande is the emperor without dresses, but Prince Manuel Valls doesn't have one either, they are forced to form a new government if the old hasn't even survived a summer.
"And the new one won't survive the autumn or winter either," Miss Le Pen told supporters.
She reiterated her party's reputation for a dissolution of parliament and said she was confident to emerge victorious in early elections and was ready for government.
France's ruling party suffered a defeat in March's local elections and the right-wing National Front was ahead in May's EU elections.
A poll in the Sunday newspaper Journal du Dimanche found that 76 percent of French people think the Socialist Party is in danger of disintegrating into several rival factions before Hollande's 2017 term ended.
Pascal Perrineau, a political scientist at Sciences Po University, warned that the French would quickly lose patience if the new socialist government fails to improve the economy and record unemployment figures.
"There's a small window for possibilities, but public opinion must quickly feel that things are changing," he warned.
Otherwise, the situation could deteriorate further.
Norwegian Cruise just before a deal with Prestige Cruises of $3bn
Norwegian Cruise Line Holdings Ltd NCHL.O, the world's third-largest crusading provider, is conducting advanced negotiations to acquire competitor Prestige Cruises International Inc for about $3 billion, according to connoisseurs in the industry.
A deal would give Norwegian Cruise, a $6.8-billion-market company, access to Prestige Cruises' luxury cruise ships as well as its wealthy clientele, while competing with larger rivals such as Royal Caribbean Cruises Ltd (RCL.N) and Carnival Corp (CCL.N).
A settlement could be announced as early as this week, the sources said on Sunday, warning that the talks could also end inconclusively.
The owner of Prestige Cruises, the private equity firm Apollo Global Management LLC (APO.N) also owns a 20 percent stake in Norwegian Cruise.
The sources asked to remain anonymous because the negotiations weren't public.
Representatives of Norwegian Cruise and Prestige Cruises did not respond to requests for comments, while a spokesman for Apollo declined to comment.
Based in Miami, Norwegian Cruise operates 13 cruise ships on routes between North America, the Mediterranean Sea, the Baltic Sea, Central America and the Caribbean.
His income stood at $2.57 billion in 2013, an increase of 13 percent compared to 2012.
Presty Cruises, also based in Miamia, operates under the brand names Oceania and Regent, which jointly operate eight cruise ships on routes to Scandinavia, Russia, the Mediterranean, North America, Asia, Africa and South America.
It reported revenue for 2013 of $1.2 billion, an increase of 6 percent year-on-year.
The cruise industry in total is about $29 billion and in the next few years it is expected due to the strengthening of the middle class in emerging markets such as China and India.
The companies are trying to position themselves for these new customers as the best choice among cruise operators.
Prestige Cruises applied to the U.S. regulators for the IPO in January 2014.
Apollo is the majority shareholder at the company since a $850 million deal in 2007.
Norwegian Cruise originated in its current form in 2000 through a merger with a cruise operator owned by Genting Bhd (GENT.KL), a recreational and casinome group under the control of Malaysian billionaire Lim Kok Thay.
Apollo invested €1bn in Norwegian Cruise in 2008.
Norwegian Cruise went public in January 2013.
Genting held 28 percent of the shares at the end of June according to the licensing authorities, Apollo 20 percent of the shares and the private equity company TPG Capital LP an 8 percent stake in the company.
Carnival, Royal Caribbean Cruises and Norwegian Cruise jointly have 82 percent of passenger capacity on American cruise ships, according to the original application documents for Prestige Cruises' IPO.
Pro-Hong Kong activists denounce China the day after the election decision.
A beijing-faithful group waved the Chinese flag nearby.
The National People’s Congress standing committee on Sunday passed a framework law allowing only two or three candidates for Hong Kong’s leadership election in 2017.
All candidates must first achieve the majority support of a nomination committee that is expected to be staffed with beijing-loyal members.
This decision makes it almost impossible for opposition Democrats to be admitted to the election, prompting pro-democracy activists to live up with their threats and paralyze Hong Kong financial centre with "Occupy Central" protests.
Political reforms have long been a contentious issue in Hong Kong; and China's party leadership fears that calls for democracy will spread to other cities.
Following the publication of a Beijing White Paper outlining China's supremacy over Hong Kong in June, democracy activists held an unofficial referendum on electoral proceedings in the Special Administrative Region, during which hundreds of thousands marched into the city's business district and organized a sit-in there.
Li's interrogation is organized by the government in Hong Kong and the Chinese liaison office in Hong Kong.
The deputy chairman of the legal commission of the standing committee, Zhang Rongshun, as well as the vice-director of the Office of Hong Kong Affairs and Macaus of the State Council, Feng Wei, should be questioned during the course of the day.
Student activists announced that they would gather in front of Hong Kong's office this afternoon.
Until the final phase of the 150-year-old colonial government in Hong Kong, Britain did not envisage any democratic elements.
Putin requires Kiev to resume "statality talks" with eastern Ukraine
Russian President Vladimir Putin demanded Russian media on Sunday that the Ukrainian government cease the fighting against the separatists in the east of the country and immediately begin negotiations on the "statehood" of the breakaway region.
His spokesman Dimitry Peskov later clarified that Putin did not mean that the territory of eastern Ukraine, which is under the control of the separatists, would become a part of Russia, but that his status towards Ukraine had to be redefined in order to give the Russian-speaking region the power to represent his rights and interests.
Putin’s call to the Kiev government to negotiate on an equal footing with the pro-Russian insurgents, however, fits with the strategy apparently pursued by him since the riots five months ago: aid to the separatists in taking territories and pressure on the Ukrainian government to enable the newly proclaimed Novorossiya region’s de facto independence for an alliance with Russia rather than the West.
In an interview with state-standed Channel Eins, Putin condemned Ukraine's military campaign to recapture the separatist-held areas in the eastern Donetsk and Luhansk regions, which were taken over in March and April after Moscow annexed Ukraine's Crimean peninsula on March 18.
The Kremlin and the separatists have lately referred to the occupied territory as "Novorossiya" or "Neur-Russia", a term reminiscent of the pre-revolutionary heyday of the Russian empire.
Putin pointed out that all those who, in the wake of the campaigns of Ukrainian politicians, are "capped in their illusions" for the elections on October 26, and while government groups are attacking civilian communities in separatist-helded areas "in their illusions," Itar-Tass reported.
"We must immediately start in-depth discussions, not only on technical issues, but also on the political organization of society and the state status of southeastern Ukraine, in order to safeguard the interests of the people living there," he said.
Peskov said Putin's reference to statehood must be seen in the context of the greater autonomy discussed for months by the Kiev leadership to dispel fears in Russian-speaking areas that their cultural and linguistic rights are endangered.
Only the Ukrainian government can grant the necessary autonomy to the eastern territories, Peskov said.
It's not about negotiations between Ukraine and Russia, Peskov said, "because it's not about a conflict between Russia and Ukraine, it's about an internal conflict in Ukraine."
The Kremlin spokesperson's clarification in correcting the "misinterpretation" of Putin's remarks underscored Russia's claim to leadership in dealing with the separatist rebellion in the east in contrast to Crimea's direct annexation, on which the majority of the 2 million residents are ethnic Russians.
Moscow would have far greater difficulty annexing the Donetsk and Luhansk regions of eastern Ukraine, as most of the 6.5 million residents are not Russians and surveys from the period before the conflict yielded a broad majority in favour of remaining in Ukraine.
Autonomy addressed by Russian diplomats on international forums would give regional governments in Ukraine authority to negotiate their own trade deals and foreign relations, and thus de facto give the Kremlin control over an area linking Russian territory with Crimea.
Annected five months ago in the Black Sea, it is the home port for the majority of the Russian navy, as well as an important commercial port and historic coastal region.
The regions between the Rostov region in Russia and Crimea are also rich in mines, factories and foundries that produce important components for the Russian military.
Ukrainian President Petroshenko suggested during his inauguration speech on June 7 that Ukrainian legislation, after new elections, should consider additions to the constitution, which gives the respective territories greater control over their finances and the status of languages.
But his notion of autonomy seems to stand in sharp contrast to those of the Kremlin and the separatist rebels whose armament and incitement Moscow is accused.
Putin's last call on Kiev to negotiate with the separatist leaders on an equal footinght followed after anothering by the rebels last week after Russian troops and tanks invaded eastern Ukraine via the previously peaceful region along the Azov Sea.
Russian-backed separatists took control of Novoazovsk, according to fears of Ukrainian security officials, the Russian-backed separatists took control.
That's whay massive civilian and military preparations have begun to fortify Mariupol, a 500,000-inhabitant steel industrial port located between Novoazovsk and narrow access to the Crimean peninsula.
Poroshenko spoke at a meeting of the EU leadership in Brussels on Saturday and urgently asked for acts to prevent further Russian aggression against Ukraine, a former Soviet republic that has been independent for 23 years.
"We're on the verge of a point where there's no going back," Poroshenko warned.
Thousands of foreign troops and hundreds of foreign tanks are now located on the territory of Ukraine.
The EU summit decided no concrete action, the leadership demanded the draft of further sanctions against Russia, which should come into force in an unspecified escalation of the Ukraine crisis.
Ukrainian soldiers had to withdraw from their positions in Ilovaysk after two Russian tank columns and 1,000 troops invaded the Donetsk region last week to support the besieged separatists, Colonel Andrij Lysenko, spokesman for Ukraine's National Security and Defense Rat reports said Saturday in Kiev.
The first presumably 63 Ukrainian soldiers trapped by the Russian incursion in Ilovaysk were exchanged for 10 Russian paratroopers who had been captured on Ukrainian territory a week ago, Lysenko said on Sunday.
Naked photo candal over Australian celebrities and Gabi Grecko
Geoffrey Nobilities made no secret of his annoyance over hackers who presumably stole nude pictures of his fiancée Gabi Grecko and a who's who of the A-celebs from Hollywood.
The film town is in excitement after a series of clear photos of a naked Jennifer Lawrence in a hacking scandal surrounding celebrities has surfaced on the internet.
The hacker in charge probably has 60 nude photos of Lawrence, star from "the Tributes of Panem" and superstars, including the models Kate Upton and Cara Delevingne, the singers Rihanna, Ariana Grande and Lea Michelle as well as the actress Kirsten Dunst.
Australian actresses Teresa Palmer, Emily Browning, Yvonne Strahovski, and Grecko from Melbourne reportedly also published private photos due to an iCloud gap.
On the list are 101 celebs.
Noblest oneship with Grecko last month, told Confidential: "It's disgusting."
All private letters and pictures should remain private.
It's a disgrace that private information can be stolen and passed on to others.
Grecko, currently in New York, reportedly told a news website that the hack was "disgraceful" and that those affected felt "violated."
The hacker presumably has 30 pictures of Palmer with her ex-boyfriend Scott Speedman, including two shots in which she lies on top without in a pool.
Palmer's film works include "Love is invincible," starring Liam Hemsworth.
Palmer, Chuck star Strahovski and actress Browning from Sucker Punch weren't available yesterday for comments on the chopped images.
A representative, Lawrence, told TMZ: "It's an abhorrent violation of privacy."
Actor Seth Rogen condemned the hacker in his tweet: "Posting hacked images from a cell phone is nothing more than the sale of Hehlerware."
From a purely legal point of view, the redistribution of stolen images should not be tolerated.
Cyclists, joggers and hikers took part in the 8th cycling on the Großer Feldberg yesterday and thus supported the association "Help for children with cancer".
The tour, which was an ordeal for some, represented an easy workout for the others.
In one, however, everyone agreed: it was fun.
Thomas Studanski probably slept well last night.
For the first time in his life, the head of the Oberurseler Brauhaus took a bike to Feldberg yesterday.
for the good cause.
He can easily put away the small sore muscles, especially since the participation in the 8th cycling on the Großer Feldberg in favor of the association "Help for children with cancer" (Frankfurt) also brought him a valuable insight personally.
It's a great thing.
"I know now that I can do this - great!"
Small break on the way in the applause curve?
No, if I'd stopped, it'd've been...
Like Thomas Studanski, who sits for the CDU in the Oberursel city parliament, many of the 718 participants tormented each other yesterday in early dew on mountains and came close to exhaustion, but happily on the plateau.
Others, on the other hand, found it easier to overcome the eleven-kilometre route between Hohemark and Feldberg.
Norman Dießner, for example.
The green politician from the Kreistag - red dress, red bike - appeared completely in red did something on understatement at the start ("A lot of professionals, there's the destination!"), but then proved to be a half-professional on the mountain and went after 45 minutes without shortness of breath.
He, too, crossed the track in one go, as did Nina Kuhn, press officer of the city of Oberursel, who had set out together with Frank Grüneisen.
For the two triathletes, "Feldberg cycling" was not necessarily a sporting challenge: "It's about charity, we're happy to be there - we would have made an exit today anyway," Kuhn said.
On the way they had met Paul Wenzel from Oberreifenberg.
The boy (12) had driven down to Oberursel in the morning to trample back to the height immediately afterwards.
Frankfurt's Florian Zierz had swung himself into the saddle at 9 a.m. in the morning, "making" the Feldberg twice, then rolled home with fun.
"Frying toni" also had fun.
For the head of Oberurseler Kultkiosk, bicycle tours over the Feldberg are simply part of it.
He had been targeting 40 minutes with his 6.5-kilo racing bike.
It's a crazy event.
For this purpose - I'm happy to be there," said Andreas Balzke.
The well-trained Mainzer says of himself that he is an "ambitious road cyclist."
That's what he has to be, because he's been riding the "Fixie wheel," that's also racing bikes, but without shifting...
Not only the 20 hikers, who climbed over the White Wall and the foux dance up to the summit, proved that it went up easily even without a bike, but also a jogger, who pulled a car tire with weights behind him on a cord.
Bergwacht doctor Professor Volker Lischke, who with his team, equipped with all-wheel-drive bully and quad, ensured safety, said: "I know him" from Frankfurt, who trains for a special sled trail, except that he pulls the sled himself".
The 718 participants - and thus as many as never before - had been sent to the track in groups in the morning.
Hikers first at 9.30pm.
This was followed by the first cyclists and runners at 10 a.m.
Five minutes later, the first mountain bikers started.
Bent Hansen, chairman of the "Radeln auf den Großen Feldberg", gave the starting squads and wished the participants a nice excursion.
For next year, he hopes that it will be possible to close the route to Feldberg up to the safety of the participants.
